   19	The drill was a short one, and Cael had to watch it four times before he understood it.
   20	
   21	It began with an entry, an ordinary forward step that any fighter on the hill would have recognised. Then a second step that looked like the first and was not: the weight went onto the front foot, and stayed there one count too long, so that a man watching for the burst would see it coming and set himself. Then a third count in which nothing happened at all. Then, on the fourth, the burst, from the wrong foot, at an angle the first three counts had sworn it would never take.
   22	
   23	Four counts. Three of them were lies, and the fourth was the truth arriving where the lies had said it could not.
   24	
   25	Lira got it wrong the first time. She burst on the third count, early, because every fight she had ever had had taught her that a third count was where you went. The Stone said nothing. He walked to the second cross and stood on it, and turned his heel a quarter, and walked back.
   26	
