  125	"Old books first," said Karis to the spine, as if he had asked. "The founding text. The first consolidation. The second. Each one until I can say what it says without looking, and nothing newer on the table until then. I made the bargain with the only person I can't talk round." She took her finger away. "A note for the cold term."
  126	
  127	She sat down, and opened the new ledger at its first page, and uncapped her pen, and that was the end of the visit. He left her tea where she could reach it.
  128	
  129	---
  130	
  131	By the third morning the assessment wing's queue reached the door.
  132	
  133	The intake had found the counter. A Stone first-year stood in front of Cael holding his form in both hands as if it might go off. Ahead of the Stone, two Current girls argued in whispers about a drill neither of them had run yet, and ahead of them a Mire third-year studied the notice board with great attention so that nobody would think he was waiting for anything. Cael had a floor-allocation sheet in his own hand, because evaluation-linked hours were lodged here or nowhere, and he spent the wait reading the wall.
  134	
  135	The rack by the door had seven pigeonholes. Last year the seventh had stood empty. This morning it was stacked with sheets of a pale grey-green, like the underside of a willow leaf, and the ink of the heading was still sharp from the press. *Research standing: floor and archive hours, by application.* At the foot of each sheet, in small type, was the wing's print number and a date, and the date was the week the council had voted Karis her line. Nobody on the hill had held such a standing before that week. The form had been waiting for her before she knew she would need it.
  136	
  137	The counter employed a clerk who added columns and a teaching assistant who read what was coming, and the green sheets had not come from the clerk. Cael looked at them exactly as long as a third-year looks at a form he has no use for.
  138	
  139	Behind the counter, the desk clerk sat at his ledger by the window, adding a column under his breath and losing it whenever the Current girls' whispering rose. Gault's door stood shut, with voices behind it. And behind the counter at the copying table, rising every time the queue moved to take the next sheet, the teaching assistant did the counter's work.
  140	
  141	Above the copying table, on the shelf where it had stood before Norhold, sat a small locked case with no mark on it. It had gone to Norhold and come back without anybody seeing it opened. Nobody looked at it now, and Cael did not look at it either.
  142	
  143	Seln took each form the same way. He read it from the name to the signature at an even pace. He stamped the received line. He tore off the copy-slip along its rule and handed it back. The Stone first-year got what the Mire third-year got, to the second. A man who read every line from every hand was a man nobody remembered reading anything in particular.
  144	
  145	When Cael reached the counter, Seln took his sheet and read it. Name; the Path line left blank, by rule; evaluator of record; hours; reason. The stamp came down, and the slip tore clean.
  146	
  147	"The Magister signs evaluation hours on Fifth-days," said Seln. "The schedule goes up the morning after."
  148	
  149	"Thank you for the form."
  150	
  151	"Mm."
  152	
  153	That was always all of it. Cael had his fingers on the copy-slip.
  154	
  155	Seln laid the sheet on the stack at his left hand and said the next thing to the stack.
  156	
  157	"Standing papers on the provision come up for the ledger's half-year check," he said. "Keep the documentation current." He set the edge of the stack true against the counter. "There have been senior men at the foot of the road since the recess. The office mentions it only because the office has eyes."
  158	
  159	He had already turned back toward the copying table. By the time Cael had folded the slip into his coat, the pen was in Seln's hand again and his head was down, and the Stone first-year had stepped up behind and was laying his form on the counter as if it were a sword he was surrendering.
  160	
  161	Cael went out through the wing doors and along the covered walk at the pace of a man on his way to the library. He did not let himself have the sentence until he was through the north arch.
  162	
  163	*Senior men. Keep the documentation current.*
  164	
  165	Seln charged for words the way a ferryman charges for crossings, and he had never in two years crossed for nothing. A remark about the weather from him would have been worth a morning's thought. Four sentences in a queue, and not one of them about the form in his hand, were very nearly a letter.
  166	
  167	---
  168	
  169	The north wall ran along the edge of the bluff above the river, waist-high, broad enough to sit on, with the whole valley open below it. He had counted the coats from here for two years, and from windows and wagon benches before that, ever since Greyvane. He knew every place on the wall where the angle was good. That evening he took the one by the third buttress, where a crack in the coping held water after rain and nobody else liked to sit, and waited for the bell.
  170	
  171	The heat of the day was going off the stone. Below him the bluff road went down in its switchbacks to the river street. Across the brown water, the ferry landing stood at the end of its causeway with the ferry tied up beside it for the night.
  172	
  173	Two at the landing. Two at the foot of the road.
  174	
  175	The bell went from the lecture range. Down in the valley, at the same moment, as if the bell had been rung for them, two more coats came along the river street from the direction of the town and took up the places at the road's foot, and the two who had stood there walked away toward the bridge without a word passed. At the landing it happened a breath later. It was done as neatly as a change of guard at a gate, and nobody on the river street so much as turned a head.
  176	
  177	Grey coats and good boots; that had not changed. What had changed was the way they stood.
  178	
  179	One of the pair at the road's foot was grey in the beard and leaned on a stick he did not seem to need. The other kept his hands in his coat and looked at the river. Neither had raised his eyes to the bluff since the change. The younger coats of other years had craned at the walls like visitors to a cathedral, and shifted their feet a little more each week as the place came clear to them. These two stood as if there were nothing on the hill they had not already been told about. They had the patience of men near the end of a long errand, who know the one thing they have been sent to see and do not mean to miss it.
  180	
  181	He watched until the light went, and they did not look up once.
  182	
  183	He wrote it on the wall, with the notebook flat on the coping and his back to the river.
  184	
  185	*Third evening. The change on the bell, both pairs, as every year. Different men. These have no questions about the hill; whoever sent them answered all of those first. Men with no questions get sent at the end of something, not the start. You don't post your best men to learn a gate. You post them to be standing there when it opens.*
  186	
  187	*I don't know what they're waiting for. Seln says senior men. Bracken watches the road. Vastin said a sitting.*
  188	
  189	*Three people who never guess, and none of them has guessed out loud. I'm not going to be the first.*
  190	
  191	He shut the book. Across the river the ferryman lit the lamp on the landing's post, and the two coats there stood in its light without moving, as patient as the post.

