   299	"How long do they usually take?"
   300	
   301	"Longer than this, for a small question." Bracken put the glass in his coat. "This is not a small question."
   302	
   303	He did not say anything else, and Cael did not ask him to. They stood together at the high window for a while longer, a narrow grey man and a boy with a bad hip, looking at an empty road in the sun. A cart came up out of the trees on the lowest switchback, loaded with hay, a farmer walking at the horse's head. Bracken took the glass out again, and looked, and put it away.
   304	
   305	"Hay," he said.
   306	
   307	That night Cael wrote it in Hesk's book under the honest floor's costs, which Lira had copied out for Karis three times in capitals and once more for him.
   308	
   309	*Seventh-day. Bracken at his high window with a ship's glass. Seven days he has watched the road, and he says the question isn't small. I believe him. I have never known him to round a number.*

