   217	"Under the hood, no man's grade at all," he said, without opening them. "The seat's own. It goes with the seal, whoever is wearing it. A monitoring officer on his rounds behind the senior chair, and the senior chair itself a district man." The coach went over a stone and he did not move. "They keep the minute, all three. None of them has ever decided anything. That's done somewhere else, and they write it down."
   218	
   219	Nobody answered.
   220	
   221	At the residence door, when the others had gone in ahead of them and the coach was rolling off toward the stable yard, Seln stopped on the step, and Cael stopped with him.
   222	
   223	"The office has written that kind of minute," said Seln, to the door. "Not that one. Others like it."
   224	
   225	Then he went in, at his even pace, and up the stair, and Cael stood a moment on the step by himself before he followed.
   226	
   227	*A morning,* he wrote that night at the sill. *A hood, two hands, a seal. A procurator with nothing on him. A junior at the records table who wouldn't look. A room doing exactly what the code says a room does.*
   228	
   229	*I keep looking for the person and finding the hand.*
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	He laid the year's dates along the sill two nights later, on the Seventh-day, on slips of his own.
   234	
   235	There was one slip for each. Lira's first meet off the hill, three weeks away now, on a road, against Silvers from other houses. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The next sitting at the wool exchange. And a fourth slip with nothing written on it, for the re-evaluation, whose date the seat would fix when it ruled. He set them in a row, as Karis had set out her six on the long table, and sat looking at them with Hesk's book open on his knee, and wrote.
   236	
   237	*The month closes, and for once the people I came up this hill with have each been handed something fair. Lira has her ladder, a point short, with the point named. Brom has his number, written on a docket a street from the seat. Karis has a shelf, and a key, and a girl to teach, and a line in a book with her name above it. None of it bought. None of it begged. All of it given across a counter, honestly, for once.*
   238	
   239	*Set it down tonight, before it gets complicated. Whatever this challenge costs me, it should not cost them.*
   240	
   241	Karis came up the stair while he was sanding it, reading her draft aloud to herself as she climbed, the way she did when a sentence would not sit. He heard her in the doorway. *The respondent submits that the integrity sections, read with their own proviso,* and then nothing.
   242	
   243	He did not turn round. He heard the draft's paper stop rustling, and her breath close behind his right ear, and he knew where her eyes had gone. His hand moved to close the book, from a habit older than the bluff, and stopped, and lay flat on the sill beside it instead.
   244	
   245	A finger came past his shoulder, ink on its second knuckle, and rested on the sill beside his last line without touching the page.
   246	
   247	"That is not how any of us are built," said Karis.
   248	
   249	It was her findings voice, the flat one.
   250	
   251	He took up the pen again and wrote under the line, in the parenthesis the Log kept for things said aloud at the window, and she watched every letter of it go down.
   252	
   253	*(Karis read this entry over my shoulder and said "that is not how any of us are built." Noted. Filed. Afraid of it.)*
   254	
   255	When the pen reached the last three words, her finger on the sill tapped once.
   256	
   257	"Afraid of it," she said. "Good." She took her finger off the sill. "Then be afraid of it accurately. Not at three in the morning, in general, about everything. Give it dates. Give it a column. Every week, put down what it's cost each of us, in the coin it actually costs, and add it up at the foot." She laid the draft on the sill beside his four slips. "Then on the day it starts costing us more than we can carry, you'll know which day it was. Not some other day. That one." She looked at the blank fourth slip. "That's what the notebook's for. Not for being frightened in. For knowing exactly how much."
   258	
   259	She went to the door, and stopped there, with her hand on the frame.
   260	
   261	"Read the draft first," she said. "Before anybody else does. That's the arrangement."
   262	
   263	"That's the arrangement," said Cael.
   264	
   265	She went down the stair. He sat a long while at the window with the four slips and her draft on the sill, and the blank slip at the end of the row, and the fountain going on below in the dark court. Then he turned to a clean page at the back of Hesk's book, past the minute and the costed rule, past the first pole and every entry since, and ruled a column down it, and wrote four names at the head of the column.
   266	
   267	He left the rest of it empty, for now. It was the first page he had ever begun that he hoped he would never need to fill.

