   227	She stood with that in the fleece smell of the old sample passage. "Then it's like a bout," she said slowly, "where the judges give you every exchange and write down you won every one, and then tell you the bout goes on anyway, under rules they'll pick later." She let go of his sleeve. "That's both. That's what she wrote on the slip, isn't it."
   228	
   229	"I think so."
   230	
   231	"I hate it," said Lira, with deep satisfaction at having understood it, and went to help Brom with the second case.
   232	
   233	Withrow came through the passage a moment later, pulling on her gloves, on her way to her carriage. She did not stop. As she passed Cael she said, without turning her head, "Sit up straight in the coach. The town watches it," which was what she had said to him on the evening of the charter folder, and went on out to the steps.
   234	
   235	Through the far doorway Cael could see the records table. The officer with the careful face had stayed at it. He was squaring the minute-book to the table's edge with both hands, and then, when it was square, squaring it again.
   236	
   237	---
   238	
   239	Brom unfolded the slip at the first milestone past the town and held it up so that they could all read it.
   240	
   241	One word, in Karis's hand. *Both.*
   242	
   243	Karis was looking out of the window at the river, and did not look round.
   244	
   245	Then for a while everybody talked about the steps at once, and nobody agreed on what had happened first.
   246	
   247	Brom said the first thing he remembered was the bruise: something had hit him hard on the left hip on the second step, and when he looked down there was a boy's satchel buckle going away from him round the corner by the corn chandler's, and a voice saying sorry over its shoulder. He had not seen the boy's face. He rubbed the hip now, thoughtfully, as if the bruise might tell him more.
   248	
   249	Cael asked Karis what the shelf had established. "The clause survived five challenges," she said. "Greyvane was the newest."
   250	
   251	Karis said Jent had stopped by her, not the counsel.
   252	
   253	"He said, 'Advocate. The ninety-one. Did you count them yourself?'" She was still looking at the river. "I said every one. He said, 'I thought so,' and put on his hat, and walked off up the street as if he were going home to his dinner."
   254	
   255	"He'll give more away next time," said the counsel from her corner, writing. "Giving things away is how that man walks forward." She wrote one line more, and Cael read it upside down: *Valid: held. To proceed: held. Both.* She closed the minute-book on it.
   256	
   257	The river went by. Lira kept one hand on the closed drill-book and never lifted its cover. Brom put the slip back in his right inside pocket and left his hand over it.
   258	
   259	At the ferry the coach waited its turn on the landing, and Cael looked out at the two grey coats who stood there as they always stood, changing on the bell. One of them was not looking at the coach's door or at the faces in its windows. He was looking at the luggage rail on the roof, where the house's two document cases were strapped, the way a clerk looks at a line he means to copy.
   260	
   261	Nobody in the coach said anything about it. All of them had seen it.
   262	
   263	The ferry took them across. On the far landing, while the coach was hauled up the ramp, Karis turned from the window at last.
   264	
   265	"We won every point that can be won on paper."
   266	
   267	Brom took the time he always took. "And?"
   268	
   269	"And it's proceeding."
   270	
   271	Cael set the night's entry in his head as the wheels found the road. It came out at one line, and he did not try to make it longer.
   272	
   273	*Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.*
   274	
   275	Seln had not opened his eyes since the town. He did not open them now. When he spoke, it was to the whole coach, and not loudly.
   276	
   277	"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."

