     1	# Chapter 30 — The Upper Ferry
     2	
     3	The clerk from the district seat found Havel at the inn by the bridge on the Third-day, a little before noon, reading the second manual for the fourth time that week.
     4	
     5	He was a young man in a wet hat, and he apologised three times on the stair before he got to the door, and twice more inside it. The district seat's records room was a man short, he said. Two men, if you counted the one with the fever. A letter had come in from the registrar of the academy on the bluff, about an order and a seal, and it concerned the proceedings, and the senior records officer had said that the proceedings' own records support was in the town on its days off with nothing to do, and would the junior chair be so good as to check the registers and draft the usual.
     6	
     7	"The usual," said Havel.
     8	
     9	"The usual, sir." The young man held out a packet in oilcloth. "With the senior's compliments. He says there's no hurry before the Fourth-day evening."
    10	
    11	Havel took it. He thanked the young man, who apologised again for the rain and went.
    12	
    13	Inside the oilcloth were two things. The registrar's letter, in a clear upright hand that wasted nothing, asking that the instrument constituting a certain office be supplied, together with the seal under which that instrument was itself issued. And a sheet of thin paper with a pencil rubbing on it of a seal, taken carefully, the grey of the lead built up in even strokes until the device stood out white: the registry's own pattern, the river and the scale, inside a beaded rim.
    14	
    15	The letter said where the rubbing had come from, in its first paragraph, with the instrument quoted whole: it was the seal off the escort order that the leader of four riders had read aloud in the house's outer court on the Sixth-day morning, and that the house had declined at its own gate and let ride away again down the bluff road.
    16	
    17	He laid the rubbing on the table by the window and looked at it for a long time without touching it.
    18	
    19	His right hand ached at the web of the thumb, as it had every morning for a year. He opened it and closed it twice under the table, out of habit, though there was nobody in the room to see.
    20	
    21	It was a good rubbing, and it was also a good seal. That was the first thing he noticed and the thing he went on noticing all afternoon: whoever had cut the die had cut it properly. The river ran the right way; the scale's beam was level. The beading round the rim was even and close, and in the beading, at the bottom, where every registry die carried its cutter's number, there were four small figures that the pencil had picked out faintly, as a pencil does when it passes over something raised. He took the reading glass out of his kit and bent over them until he was sure of all four.
    22	
    23	The usual was a letter of two sentences. He could have written it in his sleep. But the usual came after the registers, and so, because it was the order the manual gave, he went to the registers first.
    24	
    25	He checked it three ways, because there were three registers, and he did not think of it as anything more than that.
    26	
    27	The first was the district's own impression book. It lived in the records room at the district seat, on a slanted desk of its own under the window, a book as tall as a child and as thick as a door-step. Every die issued for use in the district went into it on the day of issue, pressed into a red wafer and pasted on the page in the order it came, with the office's name and the date beside it in the keeper's hand. Havel turned its pages for two hours on the Third-day afternoon with the clerk in the wet hat hovering at his elbow, offering to help and being thanked. He found the seat's own seal on its page, forty years old, the river and the scale. He found the fee office's, and the bridge-ward's, and a ferry company's, and a hundred and eighty others.
    28	
    29	He did not find the rubbing's.
    30	
    31	That proved nothing. A facilities office constituted for a proceeding might very well be a continental office and not a district one, and then its die would never have been issued here at all.
    32	
    33	So the second was the registry's printed schedule of the seals of office, which was continental. It came out every year in a stiff grey binding, with a plate for every office that had a seal, engraved to the size of the die. The district seat kept the current year and the four before it on a shelf behind the senior records officer's chair. Havel took down the current year and went through it plate by plate, the next morning, with the rubbing beside him on a clean blotter. Then he went through last year's, in case. Then he took down the current year again and went through the index of offices, under *F*, and under *P* for *proceedings*, and under *S* for *seat*.
    34	
    35	The rubbing was not in it.
    36	
    37	The third was the number.
    38	
    39	Every die the registry used was cut by one of three cutters, and every cutter numbered every die in the beading of its rim, and every number went into the cutters' issue ledger against the office the die was cut for. The ledger itself was in the west. A copy was kept at every district seat, in the strong-room, and brought up to date every quarter by the post. Havel signed for the strong-room key at noon on the Fourth-day, with the clerk as his witness, and went down with a lamp.
    40	
    41	The ledger ran in numbers, in order, one die to a line, every number cut and every number entered. That was the point of it. He found the place where the rubbing's four figures ought to be, and laid his finger on the page.
    42	
