   239	Nobody had decided how many to read. Karis read records and Brom read orders, and after a while Lira took orders too, so that Brom could rest his hand. The voices went on across the bare cloth in the circle of the lamp, very low. *Reads into. Something of. Second family, faint. Enter. Margin reading not to be entered.* Station after station went by, a mill town and a ferry crossing and a district with a name Cael had once seen on a map, a girl and a boy and a girl, and on every back the narrow slip, the nine weeks, the nothing.
   240	
   241	At the twenty-third pair Karis stopped.
   242	
   243	There was something folded behind it, under the clip. A sheet folded small, in quarters, and then another sheet folded inside that.
   244	
   245	"There's a letter," she said.
   246	
   247	Lira put out her hand.
   248	
   249	Karis looked at her a moment, and gave it to her. Lira unfolded the outer sheet on her knee and smoothed it flat with the side of her hand. It was Seln's copying hand again, but the copying hand had done something Cael had never seen it do: it had kept the shape of another man's writing inside its own. The lines ran uphill and the capitals were too large. The spelling was a careful man's spelling, a man who wrote seldom and had looked up the hard words.
   250	
   251	Lira read it aloud.
   252	
   253	"*To the clerk of the district office. Sir. I am the father of the girl entered at the river station on the ninth. I do not write to complain and I hope you will not take it so. At the frame the keeper told us she read strong and read into a second family besides, and he said he had not seen it often, and shook my hand. Now the paper has come and it says only the one, and the first rank. I expect there has been an error in the copying, which I know is easy done. I do not mind which it is. She is a good girl either way. Only she asks me, and I do not know which to tell her. If you would write and say which, I would be grateful. Your servant.*"
   254	
   255	There was no name. Seln had copied everything but the name.
   256	
   257	Lira laid the sheet on the table. Her hand stayed on it a moment.
   258	
   259	Then she unfolded the second sheet, the one that had been inside the first. It was short. She looked at it, and did not read it, and held it out across the table to Cael.
   260	
   261	He took it. Four lines, in Seln's hand again, and under them a district office's mark. He read them aloud because she wanted him to, and because he did not think he could have read them silently and kept his face.
   262	
   263	"*Sir. The entry is the registry's and I am not able to alter it.*
   264	
   265	"*I have looked at the station's sheet. The keeper's reading is on it, and it is not what the registry entered.*
   266	
   267	"*I have asked my office why, and I have had no answer, and I do not expect one.*
   268	
   269	"*I am sorry. I would tell her both.*"
   270	
   271	He put the sheet down on top of the father's.
   272	
   273	The lamp burned. Across the court, very faint through the shutters and four floors down, somebody in the residence was laughing at something, the long helpless laugh of a first-year who has been told a joke after the bell and cannot stop.
   274	
   275	"He told the truth," said Brom at last.
   276	
   277	"He did," said Karis. Her voice was quite steady. "He looked at the station's sheet himself and found the difference. He asked above him, and wrote down that he'd been given nothing. He told a man he'd never met the one honest thing he had." She touched the edge of the clerk's four lines with one finger, very lightly, the way she touched the founders' volumes. "And he sent it up with the letter, because that's what you do with a letter about an entry. You send it to the office that made the entry."
   278	
   279	She looked at the case, open at the end of the table.
   280	
   281	"And it came here."
   282	
   283	Cael sat and looked at the four lines.
   284	
   285	He had told himself all year, and the year before, that the room was the trouble and not the hill. He had found out on the Ostrand road that the room had never been the system at all. He had thought that was the bottom of it, and it was not. Here was a man who had sat in one of the rooms and done everything right. He had read the sheet and asked, and written down the answer he had not been given. He had told a father the truth in four lines, which was more than most people managed in forty. And all of it, his honesty and the father's careful spelling both, had gone up a stair into the drawer that held the corrections. It had been filed there beside them, clipped, in order, and kept.
   286	
   287	The honest people were in the drawer too.
   288	
   289	Seln took his hands off the table at last. He looked at the two sheets lying on top of each other, the father's and the clerk's, and then he looked at Lira.
   290	
   291	"That is the only letter," he said. "In forty. I copied it because it was the only one. I have read it more often than anything else in the case."
   292	
   293	Lira nodded. She folded the father's letter in quarters along its old folds and put the clerk's inside it, the way it had been, and slid them both back under the clip of the twenty-third pair, and squared the pair with the side of her finger to the one beside it.

