   269	Down in the paved court the porter was feeding the pigeons from his paper bag, as he did at this hour, and the birds had come to him in their usual half-circle. The window on the Archmarshal's left stood open a hand's breadth, and his left hand, which did not like mornings, lay on the blotter.
   270	
   271	He did not open the lowest drawer. There was no finding to consult and no minute to draft, and he had known since a borrowed room in Ostrand exactly what he would write when this leaf came. He had said it aloud there, to five people, so that he would not be able to pretend afterward that he had not.
   272	
   273	He found, now that it had come, that he did not need the saying; he would have written the same if nobody had ever heard him.
   274	
   275	He dipped the pen.
   276	
   277	He wrote on the ruled line, in the upright hand he kept for instruments, with no heading and no reason.
   278	
   279	*The Archmarshal declines to certify.*
   280	
   281	He looked at it for as long as the ink took to lose its shine. Five words. It gave no ground and cited no section and accused nobody of anything. The form had asked a question, and that was the answer to it, and there was no line on the leaf for why.
   282	
   283	He knew what would be done with it. The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld; he had cited that section himself, once, long ago, when a guild hall's assessor would not sign. His line would stop nothing. The sittings would sit, and the ruling would be read, and somewhere in a minute a clerk would enter that a thing usually present was not there. That was the whole of what he could do, and he had weighed it on a post road for four days and found that it was still worth doing.
   284	
   285	He signed beneath, his name and his office, as he had signed something over nine thousand findings, and he sanded it and shook the sand back into its box.
   286	
   287	Then he laid the leaf in the tray marked *done*.
   288	
   289	His clerk came for the tray at the usual hour. He took up the day's finished paper against his chest, and his eyes went down the top leaf once, as a good clerk's eyes do, and stopped for the space of a breath, and went on.
   290	
   291	"For the noon dispatch, sir?"
   292	
   293	"With the rest," said the Archmarshal.
   294	
   295	The clerk waited a moment, as though there might be a covering note, or a word. There was not. He went down the too-steep stair with the tray, and the Archmarshal heard him go, every step, to the bottom.
   296	
   297	He drew the next thing toward him from the tray on the right, a guild hall's query about a frame. He read it through from its head to its seal, and dipped his pen, and answered it.

