   306	Cael had been in a great many himself by now. He had been in a wool exchange with a lot number under his boot, and a dairy with a slit window, and a borrowed office with nothing of anybody's in it; he had stood on a north road outside a room he would never go into; and he had been careful in every one of them.
   307	
   308	He looked down at sixty feet of sprung oak in a hole in the ground, where for an hour that afternoon he had not been careful with anything.
   309	
   310	It was a yard, and had no roof. He said it anyway.
   311	
   312	"Good room."

