Not fixed, for the ledger: the landing beat is "three years" at the year's open and "four years" at its close (ch1, ch60), by the map's N9; Withrow's chancellorship is at least eighteen years (ch23, ch28, ch31) beside Book 4's twenty registry years; Vastin's room is nineteen years his; Ephram was two years at the head of the Iron column; the Tide anomaly is touched three times (ch1, ch45, ch60); the office's "three weeks" is the only span given for the sitting; Hesk's clock runs an hour fast from the crossroads to finals morning; the keeper is "he"; Lira's shoulder is the left; the slip count is two; the four initials on the costed rule are in Hesk's book under the minute; the six guesses are written on the night of the closing and Auremont's four words print the morning after; no Norhold session (C8) was drafted, so two renewals stand at the close; Seln's cases are two from fix 6 on, the locked one and a pen-case.

## 9. Three cross-movement priorities for one same-author pass in two lanes

1. **Thin the repertoire (both lanes, every chapter).** Items 1–10 of §3. Scope: one reading pass, sentence by sentence, no script; one squaring and one "flat" a chapter, and let the gesture belong to Seln and Bracken again; cap the superlative-ever at five for the book (§3.2 names them); cut "as he read/did/considered everything" to its first instance per character; "for a moment", "Nobody said anything" and "said nothing" only where the pause is the point; "a fraction" once a bout and "a stride" sharing with "a pace" and feet; the elevens that are not canon moved to other numbers (lane A: ch3, ch4, ch18, ch25, ch28; lane B: ch33, ch44, ch51, ch53, ch56, ch60's "eleven of them now", keeping the twelve-verses-to-eleven joke); Ephram's ears to three; the crowd simile to one a bout; "rain on a roof", the pond and the dog each once; vary or drop the "He wrote it that night" frame while keeping the entries; and let two or three exhibitions end on the flag without a handshake aphorism. Expect to lose two to three thousand words and to get the characters back by ear.
2. **Economise the exhibition shape (lane A, ch12, ch18, ch21, ch23; lane B, ch51).** Scope: in ch21 the merry Blade to a paragraph and the Log to its bill lines; in ch23 the coastal Wind as it is and the Log to its bill lines; in ch18 the audit to the two rules it adds (the pouch and the closed floors) rather than the whole inventory of what the market holds; in ch12 nothing but the Log; in ch51 the first exchange's recap of the semifinal's shapes to a sentence. Five to nine hundred words in lane A, two to three hundred in lane B. No scene breaks added or removed; no bout cut; the captain and the barge-master untouched.
3. **Settle the seams and update the ledger (both lanes).** Apply fixes 1–10 (and 11, or lane B's own line for Fiske); then reread ch53 and ch58 once for the two cases, ch31 and ch59 for the guard, ch27 for the flag, and ch1 beside ch60 for the two year counts, to confirm every remembered figure points at a first occurrence. Record in the ledger what §8's "not fixed" paragraph lists, and carry to the Book 6 reconciliation the hand-off items in §1: the two renewals and A11; "two watchers and a horse" at a bridge; Withrow's eighteen years and Vastin's nineteen; the flag standing at the close; the summons at ninety days.

## 10. Close to source — passages that read as source-shaped

Not a probe; a reader's note for the lanes, since M8 reads 11% and M9 15% close by the tool. Three stretches carry the source's beat order so plainly that a listener hears an outline under the prose: the panel's night told in the pluperfect (ch57:51–157, "It had begun with a handcart… Then his own clerks had read out…"), where every argument is reported rather than heard; Daeva's history (ch49:103–157, the garden, the registry page, the programme, "Specimen.", the coast exercise), which is a summary of the source's window with its beats in the source's order, however fine the sentences; and the convening (ch60:109–129), which is a window by design and stays inside it. Two smaller ones: the road sorting itself (ch28:83–121) and the aftermath list after the lane (ch56:105–117, the barrel, the ledgers face down, the tower stairs), both of which read as the source's catalogue rendered. None of them is a close-band problem the tool would flag at the sentence; all of them are places a same-author lane could put a scene where there is a report.

