    55	It came up with the evening post — the estate's mark in the wax, the hand the delegation had seen twice a year for two years and once, at Norhold, on a single line — and he did not take it to the courtyard. He took it upstairs, and it lay on the small table in his room — Lira reported this, because Lira reported things — for two days, unopened, beside the docket, the two envelopes squared together with the care he gave to useful things and neither of them touched. Lira reported it the way Lira reported things, which was as logistics, at the residence table, to nobody in particular, with the drill-book open in front of her and her eyes on the page. "Second envelope's still on his table. Squared with the docket. Two days." She turned a leaf she was not reading. "He moved the lamp so it isn't in the light."
    56	
    57	"You went into his room," said Karis.
    58	
    59	"I went past his door. Twice a day, because the stair goes past his door." She turned another leaf. "I'd like it noted that I didn't go in, which for me is growth, and that I wanted to, which for me is honesty."
    60	
    61	"Noted," said Cael.
    62	
    63	"Don't note it on paper. Note it in your head." She closed the drill-book. "And when he opens it, nobody asks. Not one of us. Not even the researcher." She looked at Karis when she said it, without heat, the way you name the risk in a room to the person carrying it.
    64	
    65	"I wasn't going to ask," Karis said. "I was going to sit where he could tell me if he wanted to. There's a difference and I have learned it entirely from watching him do it to me."
    66	
    67	Brom came down the stair at that point, and the table's conversation changed direction without anybody steering it — the term's schedule, the second session's date, whether the intake's rota had been disbanded — and he ate, and said four words about the schedule, and went back up, and the envelope stayed on the table by the lamp for one more day.
    68	
    69	On the third evening he opened it alone, and nobody at the table ever learned its contents, because he showed nothing of them; what the table got was one fact about his face when he came down to dinner afterward, catalogued from across the room at the instrument's full attention:
    70	
    71	It was the face of a man being offered his whole ledger back.
    72	
    73	Not joy. Not grief. The specific stillness of a person handed a document that contains everything he had ever been priced out of — the household, the standard, the Gold-tier arithmetic that had made a big careful boy a rounding error at fourteen — with a line at the bottom inviting his signature, and a date. Cael had seen the face once before, on Lira, at a registry station's anteroom, holding a folded certification that said *Iron*. He had not seen it on Brom. He filed it, and did not ask, and wrote the only margin the arrangement permitted: *the docket says Iron by term's end. The letter says something that arrived in the same week and got two days on a table before he'd touch it. Two envelopes. One ledger. He's being offered the number and the family in the same fortnight, and he has said "Noted" about neither, which from Brom means the account is open and the arithmetic is running. Wait for the Fifth-day. Don't renegotiate.*
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	Karis's standing spent its budget for the first time that month, and Cael was present for the spending because she made him present for it.
    78	
    79	The budget line was, in the academy's arithmetic, small — a research allocation for copying and courier and archive requests, the kind of sum a faculty member spent without noticing — and she spent the first of it on a request to Greyvane's archive for a certified copy of the founding-era compilation's oldest signatures, the ones Prynn had once said would be there when she was forty. The request went down the bluff road under the academy's seal, with a budget code on it, and Karis stood at the counter watching Bracken enter the code with the expression of a woman who had spent three institutions asking for things on her own account and had just, for the first time, asked on an institution's.
    80	
    81	"They'll fill it in three weeks," Bracken said. "Prynn's people are slow and exact. You'll have your signatures before midwinter."
    82	
    83	"Before midwinter," Karis repeated, and wrote the date in the notebook's front matter, where the citations lived, and Cael saw the page and saw the space she had left beneath the date, and did not ask what it was for.
    84	
    85	The assistant — a second-year named on no roster the delegation kept, because Karis kept her the way Vell had kept apprentices, by role — learned her fourth error of the month that afternoon, at the annex table, and Cael charted the teaching from the window because the teaching was itself a datum: Karis correcting a reading of the integrity provisions by declining to explain the correction, handing over the volume, and saying "find it," and the girl finding it in twenty minutes with her jaw set, and Karis saying "yes" and nothing else, and the girl going faintly pink with the specific pleasure of having been treated as competent. *She's building someone,* the log noted. *The institution gave her a room and a budget and a person, and she's using the person the way she'd use the room — as a thing to be made better than it was. Three years of watching Vell and Rooke and Quenna do it to us. It transferred. Note that everything the system gave her honestly, she is spending honestly, which is the only kind of debt that can be called in.*
    86	
    87	---
    88	
    89	Ephram trained beside Brom that month, at the striking frames, and Cael watched the two of them from the second floor's rail on four separate evenings before he understood what he was watching.
    90	
    91	Not sparring. Not the cohort captain drilling the continental Copper champion, which would have been the natural reading and was wrong. The two of them worked the frames side by side in the same rhythm, a hair apart — Brom running the defensive structure top to bottom the way he ran it before every long road, Ephram running Blade doctrine at the cohort's speed beside him — and they did not speak, and the not-speaking had a shape. Ephram would stop, and watch Brom's reset, and resume. Brom would stop, and watch Ephram's entry line, and resume. Two men taking each other's measure with total thoroughness, night after night, the way you learn a floor you expect to fight on.
    92	
    93	"He's deciding something," Cael said to Lira, at the rail.
    94	
    95	"He decided it at Norhold," Lira said. "He's waiting for the right term to say it. Ephram doesn't say things early; he catches it from Rooke." She watched them. "Whatever it is, it's about the two of you, and it's about a floor, and he's going to ask for it properly, and you're going to say yes, and I'm going to be there."
    96	
    97	"You've read this."
    98	
    99	"I've read Brom's face while he watches Ephram's entry line. It's the face he had on the road from Ardenmere the week before he told you the story about Velmere." She pushed off the rail. "People who don't say things have a tell for when they're about to. It's that they get very good at their work."
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	Karis convened the annex the night before the first session, inner circle only, one lamp, and laid the session's procedure out on the table the way she had once laid out a tournament's scoring architecture: who sat where, what each seat was permitted to do, which of the code's provisions governed the entering of a petition and the entering of a response and the silence between them. "The seat will receive both," she said. "It will not rule on either tomorrow. It will confirm the schedule. The whole session is a form being filled in by four people in a room, and I want everyone at this table to have seen the form before they see the room, so that nobody mistakes the room for the thing that matters."
   104	
   105	"What's the thing that matters?" Lira asked.
   106	
   107	"The seal," said Karis. "Watch the seal. Watch what it does when the counsel enters eleven pounds of the truth. If it does anything at all, I'll revise the corridor."
   108	
   109	---
   110	
   111	The proceedings' first session convened at Ostrand on the fourth Fifth-day of the month, and it was procedural, cold, and brief, and it was the coldest hour of Cael's institutional life, because nothing in it was aimed at him.
   112	
   113	The morning began at the side door, which was the day's first instruction in what he had become.
   114	
   115	The delegation coach put them down at the foot of the market street at the half-bell, and the four steps of the wool exchange had thirty-one people on them — Cael counted them from the coach window before the door opened, because counting was cheaper than deciding how to feel — of whom nine were correspondents with folding boards and the rest were Ostrand's registry-literate come early for a bench. The side entrance had a registry officer and a list. The officer read the list, and read Withrow's name off it, and the counsel's, and Karis's as *documentary support*, and Brom's and Lira's as *institutional attendance*, and Seln's as *records support, Halcenvane*, and then came to the last line and read it aloud, which he did not have to do and did because the code said parties were identified on entry.
   116	
   117	"The enrollee of record."
   118	
   119	"Present," said Cael.
   120	
   121	"Name for the door log?"
   122	
   123	"There isn't one on the instrument," the counsel said, before Cael could answer. "The instrument says *the enrollee of record, unclassified.* Enter what the instrument says."
   124	
   125	The officer entered what the instrument said. It took eleven seconds. Cael watched the pen do it and filed them where the other eleven seconds lived, and went in through a door that had been opened for the term because the ordinary one was not built for the people who had come to watch.
   126	
   127	The registry seat's proceedings chamber was the exchange's old selling floor, cleared: a long room with the lot positions still cut into the boards, the registry's sigil on the end wall, the presiding seat on a low platform beneath it, the evaluation seat west of the seal, the records table behind the platform and below it, the two parties' tables facing — petitioner to the northwest, respondent to the northeast — and a public gallery of forty benches that the correspondents filled to the rails. Cael counted the room the way he counted every room, and the count came back with the same absence in it three times.
   128	
   129	The presiding seat was occupied. A registrar in the registry's senior grey with the seat's hood forward, so that there was no face in it to read at all — and Cael pointed the whole instrument at the posture and the hands for an hour and got a reading he entered that night with the flat care he kept for failures: *the hands rest where the code puts them and move when the procedure moves them: the minute, the schedule, the receipt. No adjusting, no waiting, no weight shifted on the bench in forty minutes. Four years of reading men who were managing their faces, and it turns out the face was a courtesy. This one isn't managing anything, because there is nothing being managed. A posture, doing a seat's work.* The record identified the seat by seal. The clerk read the seal's designation into the minute at the session's opening and the registrar did not add a name, because the code did not require one, and the whole long room proceeded to conduct the most consequential proceedings of the enrollee's life in front of a chair whose occupant was, by procedure, nobody.
   130	
   131	The gallery held the correspondents at the rail and, behind them, the ordinary curious — Ostrand's registry-literate, clerks and factors and two guild officers, come to watch a chair with no name conduct the continent's most-read case — and among them, in a plain coat, at the gallery's end where the sightlines to every table and door converged, the delegation's assessment clerk, who had come down on the coach as records support and had taken, without appearing to choose, the seat a man takes when he intends to read a room and not be read by it. Cael marked him from the respondent's table and did not look again. Withrow sat at the table's head in her registry grey with the response in its two cases beside her, and did not touch them, because a chancellor at a proceeding did not fidget with her own filing. The counsel sat with the minute-book open. Karis sat with the notebook closed, which was its own announcement.
   132	
   133	Jent entered the petition. It took nine minutes. He read the instrument's operative paragraphs into the record in the voice of a man reading a schedule, tendered the exhibit list — Umber's referred tournament file among the exhibits, the *unscorable under standard* entry now evidence — and sat down at the correct height. The counsel entered the response, which took twenty-two minutes because two hundred and six items took twenty-two minutes to summarize even at a registry pace, and the seal received both without expression, and confirmed the schedule, and adjourned.
   134	
   135	The evaluation seat was empty; its empanelment would be confirmed at a later session, the notice said, when the district's senior evaluators had been canvassed. The records table was staffed — a senior records officer and, behind him, on rotation, a junior with a private carefulness Cael knew from three postings and two academies. Havel did not look at the respondent's table once in forty minutes. From Havel, four years and five note-entries deep, the not-looking had the weight of a sentence.
   136	
   137	Withrow, at the session's close, did the one thing the code permitted a respondent chancellor and nothing more: she rose when the seal rose, and inclined her head to the seat at the depth the registry taught its officers to incline to a seat, and Cael, watching, charted the gesture's exactness — twenty years of registry courtesy, performed at a chair whose occupant she would never be able to name — and understood that she was addressing the seal on purpose, as a seal, to enter into her own ledger that she had given the institution its due and would expect the same from it. She said nothing on the stairs. In the coach she said: "I have bowed to that seat three hundred times from the other side of it. Today I learned what it looks like from this one. Note that I bowed anyway."
   138	
   139	The coach's council on the river road ran the session the way the delegation ran everything, in order of arrival. The counsel first: "Procedurally immaculate. He entered nothing he didn't need. I entered everything we had. The seat received both as if they weighed the same." Karis: "They do weigh the same. To the seat." Brom, after the length of time Brom took: "Havel didn't look at you." Cael: "No." Brom: "He looked at you at Greyvane. Twice, from the delegation gallery. I counted." Lira, to the window: "So the junior's stopped learning too." And Seln, from the corner, eyes closed, delivering the session's only finding in the register the table weighed at full freight: "The records officer at the senior chair is district grade. The junior behind him is monitoring grade, on rotation. The seat is registry-seat grade — the seal's grade, not a person's. Three grades of officer in one room and not one of them is the grade that decides anything. The office notes it." He did not open his eyes. "The office has sat in rooms like that. From the other chair."
   140	
   141	Cael filed the session's observation on the coach back up the river road, and it was the observation the whole term would keep returning to, and the log held it whole:
   142	
   143	*The hearing at Greyvane had Coss's face on it — you could read the whole case off him from across the room. The inspection had Vastin's; the evaluation was the man. This has a seal. A registrar of no age and no face reading a designation off a clerk's card, and a procurator with nothing on him, and a room conducting itself. I keep looking for the person and finding the hand.*
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	He wrote the month's close at the window, with the docket dates on the sill — Lira's meet, Brom's review, the second session, the blank — and the entry came the way honest entries came, not planned, just true enough to write down:
   148	
   149	*Everyone I came here with is holding something the system finally gave them honestly. Lira's ladder. Brom's number. Karis's shelf. Write it down now, while it's simple: whatever this challenge costs me, it should not cost them.*
   150	
   151	Karis read the entry over his shoulder — she had come to the window with a draft, and had stopped, and had read, which she did not do, and he let her, which he did not do — and said, in her flattest register: "That is not how any of us are built."
   152	
   153	He wrote that down too, underneath, in the log's own parenthesis:
   154	
   155	*(Karis read this entry over my shoulder and said "that is not how any of us are built." Noted. Filed. Afraid of it.)*
   156	
   157	Then he read the parenthesis back to her aloud, because she had stood at his shoulder long enough to have earned the hearing.
   158	
   159	She listened to it with her arms folded and the draft still in her hand, and did not soften a word of it.
   160	
   161	"Afraid of it," she said. "Good. Be afraid of it accurately — with dates, and a column, and a total at the foot — and on the day it starts costing them you will know it was that day and not some other. That is what a notebook is for."

