## 9. Reserved truths and release limits for this book
# Book 5 completion pass — Step 2: whole-arc read

Reader: Claude Fable 5.1, fresh context, standing in for Sol (Codex), whose quota is exhausted; no drafting history with this book. Date: 2026-10-06.
Read in order: COMPLETION-PLAN (with its Step 2 queue), Book 4's `state/completion/whole-arc-read.md` as the model, `STATE_LEDGER.md` (entry state, every "After Movement N" block with its coordinator rulings, every CLOSED note), BOOK_MAP (§1 entry and ending, §6 with [M6-redated], §8, §10 line by line, §11, §12), OWNER-DECISIONS #35–#42, then `manuscript/chapter-01.md` through `chapter-60.md` straight through (299,086 words by the metrics tool; 299,764 by `wc`). Book 4's ch37, ch48–49, ch53, ch57 and ch59 were grepped for the seams the queue names. Mechanical checks were run over all sixty chapters afterward: season, month and weekday words; metric measures; the forbidden vocabulary of §11; the keeper's pronoun; every year-count phrase; every §10 string; the repetition counts in §3. No manuscript file was touched and no git command was run.

## Verdict

The book holds as one arc and lands its ending. Read end to end it does what the map asked: the Standard is taught hard in the first third, three ways (Rooke's yellow card, the mock ring with Gault's spectacles, page thirty-one by a stove), so that by the mill town a reader can price a figure before the clerk chalks it, and from then on the instrument surfaces only at its turns; the qualifying season is a real season, four towns with four floors, each floor an opponent that files no challenge; the doctrine is made to work too well, is caught by a protest, a betting board, a clerk with a pencil and a grey-wool woman in a gallery, and is repaired on purpose with a programme that costs a shoulder; Norhold is a city with the roof off, and the three companions' floors (Zerin, Marek, Ivenne) are each a different problem fought to a different cost; "Silver is not a bigger Iron" is earned in a pocket of quick stone with a Silver's hand coming down; the found family fights together once, and loses to a diagonal nobody priced; the frame empties from below and fills from above with one word; and the match (ch54–56) is the best writing in the book, three exchanges of a bank being spent to the minute, a break in which the other instrument is heard working underneath the account, and a reach held to the three numbered rules of a year-old minute, in the time it takes to draw a breath, with the stumble, the mercy on a clock and the notice exactly where §10 puts them. The panel's night (ch57) earns "under" over "by" in twenty minutes on one word; the hour in the room full of clocks (ch58) is the book's quietest and most dangerous scene; the closing's eight claps that would not stop, the banner's four ties in their charter order and two clerks signing in front of eight thousand people say what the book has been saying since Bracken's sand; and the convening window stays inside its nine hundred words and its altitude. The calendar chains from the last morning of the recess to the seventeenth day of the road home without a break against #41. Every figure, score, tally and count agrees with the page and the ledger. Every protected line is present and where the map puts it. Seln is never told, Rooke never asks, Ephram is kept outside the door and knows it, Vastin never guesses, Daeva declines to, and the Architect plants sit at their altitude.

The weaknesses a straight read shows are not structural. First, the author has a repertoire, and it has shifted since Book 4 rather than thinned: the squared page (198 hits of "square/squared" in 51 chapters: "squared to the edge", "square to the table", "her small square hand"), "flat" (186), the superlative-ever formula ("the most careful thing anybody had ever done to him on a floor", 41 instances), "as he read/did/considered everything" (27), "Nobody said anything" (31) and "said nothing / did not say anything" (92), "for a moment" (120), the micro-timing coinage ("a fraction" 53, "half a beat" 36, "quarter-beat" 35), "to nobody in particular" (25), the ears going red (13, eleven of them Ephram's), and the number eleven again (68, a third of them reached for). Second, a shape that repeats until a reader can predict it: the exhibition bout with its five parts (walk the floor; find the one thing the opponent never priced; the handshake aphorism; the figure "at the proper interval"; the Log entry with the bill), run fifteen times, and at Norhold the back room with its rocking table and the pump, visited twelve times. Third, a soft stretch at the confluence (ch21–23: five exhibitions in two chapters, three of them teaching lessons the quarry town has already taught) and a flat one on the empty board (ch47). Fourth, four seams a line pass closes: a wrist "healed for a month" five months after the final; "the tournament was still nine days off" at a flag nine days from the city and sixteen from the opening; a colour-guard that reads as eight men where the ledger has four; and Seln opening "his own case" on the eve of finals when the book says the locked case was never opened. Eleven line fixes below, three cross-movement priorities for the two lanes. Nothing needs a rebuild.

**Rulings on the queued items.**
1. *The calendar* holds against #41 and BOOK_MAP §6 [M6-redated] at every dated beat (§1 below). "Three weeks" appears five times as the office's round figure (ch29, ch32, ch49, ch54, ch56) and no text states the tournament's length in days. Rooke's "Four days to the first and seven to the second" (ch32:211) is on the page once, and ch33:67–71 kills it with the deferral in Brom's mouth and Seln's "The tower has changed its opinion"; it is not restated. One line drifts and is fixed below (ch27:293).
2. *Formats.* Exhibitions: five exchanges at most, two touches ends it, a clean throw a touch, level the figure — stated at the wool town (ch7), used at the quarry town (ch19:25), restated once by Rooke at the orientation (ch30:97) and by the referee at each Norhold exhibition. Regional brackets two touches inside four, the figures deciding after four (ch6, ch16, ch22). Continental points per exchange stated once, in Karis's reading of the grey card (ch30:71), and every Norhold bout's arithmetic obeys it (§1). The team-trial format stated once by Rooke (ch45:113) and only summarised by the referee at the presentation (ch46:23) and at third place. #40: no bout for third at the confluence (ch22:183) or at Norhold (ch30:101, Gault's "fourth on the figures" ch24, ch30, ch52); three capabilities on public floors from the caravan captain on (ch26:95–97, "the record holds two of them public, and has been shown a third at its documented rate"). Every rating sits inside #38's bands: Cael's fifteen run 20–27, the captain's 30 is par at Silver and the only par on the page, Zerin's 29 is called "a Silver figure" (ch35:293).
3. *Year counts* are not incremented: the landing beat "three years" and Lira's bursts "three years" (ch1:21, :109), Compression "two years" and the anomaly "two years running" (ch1:115, :121), the watchers "two years" at both ends (ch2:189, ch60:169), Lira's "three years of work" (ch19:391), the protected "three years" of items 21 and 29 (ch13:241, ch59:43). The one incremented figure is the landing beat's "four years of trying to trim it" in the year's closing ledger (ch60:11), which BOOK_MAP §3 B.2 and the ledger's N9 license ("a year on, four"); it stands, and the ledger should record ch1 three / ch60 four as the book's own pair. Daeva is thirteen in the garden court, six years from it, seven in the programme, nineteen (ch49, ch52, ch56, ch57). Vastin is "forty years" (ch5, ch24, ch29, ch43, ch50 "forty-odd years of keeping a calendar", ch58 "forty-year career") and no age is stated; Umber is forty years at the seals (ch31, ch37, ch57); Rooke twenty coaching; Bracken twelve; Seln fifteen (M3's canon). New figures for the ledger: Withrow has "carried the name up and down the bluff for eighteen years" (ch23:243, ch28:29, :61, ch31:143), which makes her chancellorship at least the eighteen years the house has been missing and sits with Book 4's "twenty years inside that registry" and "past sixty"; Vastin has "worked in [his room] for nineteen years" (ch50:115); Ephram "two years" at the head of the Iron column (ch1:211); the quarry foreman twelve years on the terrace; the cookshop twenty-two years.
4. *Season, month and weekday words:* none. The only month names are Book 4's dated recollections (the eleventh of Sowing ch4:213; the twentieth of Reaping ch2:159, ch13:127, :135, ch33:219; the fifteenth of Reaping ch58:15), all of them dates and none beside a season word. "Spring" occurs only as the oak's spring (ch14, ch19, ch20), "sowing" once as a verb (ch56:7), "march" as the colour-guard's tune. No English weekday anywhere, including Daeva's window, where the "Tuesday calibration" is the programme's design session (ch49:5). Halcenvane's First-day to Seventh-day run from ch2 to ch26 and are right by the ledger's arithmetic at every check.
5. *Measures:* feet, yards, inches, paces, strides, miles, hands and thumbs; no metric word in the book.
6. *Names and pronouns.* The guesting-house keeper is "he" at every appearance (ch28, ch42, ch52, ch53, ch54, ch57). Lira's burst shoulder is her LEFT from the bout to the closing (ch35:209, :275, ch36:13, :95, ch37:15, ch38, ch39:223, ch40, ch45:221, ch46, ch48:187, ch52, ch59:175); the right-shoulder stiffness after the wool town (ch10:77) is M2's separate debut bill and stands. Brom is Copper formal on Ilsev's form with no rank (ch38:7–19), "continental Copper champion" (ch37, ch39, ch51, ch59). Ephram is Iron Rank Six (ch1:195). The Fenmark rater is a woman (ch59:147). Bracken is "he", twelve years, and "quarry stone" is used three times by design.
7. *The Ilsev / Havel / Vastin record against Book 4* holds. Ilsev: first referral sent up from Greyvane; returned twice with "no originating authority of record", the second return with "a third sentence latched on behind"; the second, fuller referral filled in at Halcenvane "on the fifteenth of Reaping", marked a level higher (ch58:15) — this is B4 ch49:27–75 and Havel's fourth entry ("refiled a level higher inside the recess", B4 ch49:113) and B4 ch57:225 ("gone up a level inside the first recess on the fifteenth") exactly. Her standing note "Authorizing office: not traced" (ch46:147) is B4's. Havel: four entries at Ardenmere, Greyvane and the bluff, "carried through three postings" (ch25:11, :21; ch32:319; ch58:45), the fifth written at Norhold (ch58:47–51). Vastin: the file note "Classification error is unlikely. Continue observation." quoted as his own (ch5:31) and only ever a belief in Cael's mouth ("I believe says I'm not a mistake, and I'd wager he signed it with his whole name", ch43:105; ch58:83); "the Archmarshal" throughout; the eleven questions in a room twelve by fourteen (ch5, ch24, ch42, ch43, ch58) and the fourth question (ch58:233) are B4's; his high room with the porter and the pigeons replaces the source's river-side office consistently (ch5, ch24, ch29, ch50).
8. *The ring canon and the bank minute against the match* hold to the figure. Ring: fifty-two by forty-eight (ch52:165–167; the first ring "fifty-two… a few feet less" ch40:17), the half-inch dome from the oak every way with the long road north–south (ch52:183, ch54:113), the six-foot barrier with the bull-nose cap (ch50:79, ch52:85, :171), four breaks with a cap seam felt through a boot (ch52:209, ch54:113, :161), four masts each grounded twice into a trench falling east (ch50:79, ch52:159, ch55:45, ch56:101, ch57:15), three rows taken up (ch52:85), the oak an island on its footing (ch52:79), Auremont's risk office on the sheet (ch50:81, ch52:89, ch54:57, ch57:13). The minute (ch53:179) names five things; the match spends each as minuted, only inside a fight she began, and the ledger's tally agrees with the page: Wind bursts one to three in the first exchange, the fourth to the lane's end in the second, five to seven in the third (the seventh "past six" and the hip's bill arriving early, ch55:149), eight and nine in the fourth and a tenth refused by the hip (ch56:13) — nine in all, three past six (ch60:11); the push once in the first (ch54:157, "One blow of the four"), twice more in the third (ch55:149), the fourth and fifth in the fourth, "past the four and into the minute" (ch56:21) — five (ch60:13); Reydan's give on the Gold's wash in the first ("in the book", ch54:141), drinking most of the fire in the third, full in the fourth; Ember once at the touch (ch55:61), twice more on her forearm in the third — three, one past two (ch55:149, :175; ch60:19); the read to depth, the woman or the air (ch55:111). Shadow offered in the last corner and refused (ch56:65; ch60:21). The break's inventory (ch55:169–181) is the same tally in Cael's hand. The lane is fifteen feet at a third of her depth in the ring (ch56:91), on the panel's measure (ch57:93) and in the ledger (ch60:23). The notice is verbatim and in a code block; its first words are "Gold" and a stability warning, counted as two firsts (ch56:210).
9. *Placeholders.* Gwen, Abbot, Bede and Maud do not appear in this book; nothing to rule.

## 1. Continuity across movements

### Calendar — holds, day by day
Checked every dated beat against the ledger's day-numbers and against each other. The recess: twelve weeks and four days, sixty-one sessions, nine letters, one dated change at the landing in the ninth week (ch1). The qualifying year: d0 convocation (First-day), d4 Fifth-day the filing and the counter, d5 Rooke signs Ephram's sheet, d8 the memorandum "four days after the courier", d9 the stair at a run, d10 Bracken's check, d11 Withrow signs, d12 the morning courier, d21 the fourteenth day crossed off, d16 Hesk's reply "the fourth day of the silence", d18 "the sixth evening" on the wall, d25 the card ("Thirteen days, Karis says", ch5:131, which is d12 to d25), d28 the map room "the first evening of the fifth week", d29 the mock ring, d30 "five days out" and "a little over three weeks" from the signing (ch4:153, d11 to d35), d33 the ferry, d34 the wool town, d35–36 the meet, d37 the ruling before noon, d38–39 half work, d45 Bracken on the stair, d46–52 a week west with frost on the fourth morning, d53 the mill town, d56 the yard-master, d57 the evaluation and "Variance is cheap", d59 the first pole at a coaching inn, d66 the circular "two days after the wagons got home", d67 the counter and the pouch "in two days", d69 the ferry, d77 the milestone and the silence, d78 the quarry town "at dusk on the tenth day", d81 the first meet day "eleven weeks" after d4 (ch15 and the ledger), the coat-rack "the end of the silence's fifth day", d83 the foreman and the costing "more than a fortnight ago" (d66), d84 the quarterly, d86 the waystation ("two days coming down", "another week" to the bluff, the circular at midday, the chancellor told "approximately four hours ago"), d93 the bluff on a Third-day evening, d97 the walking exchange "on the fourth morning", d101 "the second Fourth-day" on the speed line, d106 the wagons north with the carriage, d107 the confluence at dusk, d108–109 the meet, d110 the convenor, d112 the lamps on a First-day "two days later", d119 the cold term, d149 the reading room "a Third-day afternoon in the fifth week", the sixth week's letter, d186 Seln's report "the last Fifth-day", d187 the seedings, d188 the wall, "the next night" the melt, d190 departure on a Second-day. The road: day 3 the coast column, day 4 "downhill", day 6 the corner game, day 9 the flag "a week out", day 12 the five-arch bridge, day 14 the road sorting itself, day 17 the rise, day 18 the gate. Norhold: the second morning the likeness, the third evening Ephram's "pre-sold", the fourth Auremont, the fifth morning the orientation and the draw at dusk, Umber's eve, T1 "the first day of the new month", T2 the first rounds, T3 the two filings, T4 the trial rules and seat twelve, T5 Lira's quarterfinal and the deferral, T6 Ephram's exit, T7 the open warm-up, T8 Zerin and Brom's rematch, T9 Marek, Ilsev's form, Ivenne, the plaque "on the ninth day", T10 Karis's semifinal "the tenth day" and the crews, T11 the captain, T12 the cake ordered "the morning after your first bout", T13 the hired hall and Vastin by post road, T14 "the fourteenth day" and seat twelve filled "thirteen days" held, the night of seventeen, T15 the rest day, the fourth of Gault's four, the draw and the filing, T16 the trial, T17 Rhagen's news and "Twenty-first day, then, for third", "four mornings from now", T18 rain, the warehouse "on the second morning of the empty board", T19 the form "before dawn on the third day", Bracken's three troubles, the strip at dusk, the long table at the eighth bell, T20 the acceptance, the hearing at the fourth bell, the squall, Vastin's notice "the day after the filing" with departure "the morning after next", T21 the hill, the standings, the party and "three days from now", the midnight crews, T22 the shut hall, the ring "two days", T23 the fifth bell and the eve, T24 finals day, "two evenings ago" at the mast, the keeper's cap after "three weeks of mornings", T24–25 the panel, T25 the entry at dawn, the copy line, the return, the hour at the fourth bell, the pole, the rule, T26 the sixth guess "on the second morning", the closing at the second bell, the ledger "that evening", the pouch at the eighth bell, T27 Auremont's four words (as the M9 ledger corrects), road days 1, 3, 5, 9, 11 (Hesk's letter "dated four days before"), 12, 14, 17. Every Halcenvane weekday named falls where the ledger's arithmetic puts it.

One place drifts. **ch27:293**: "The tournament was still nine days off" at the crossroads flag on the ninth road day: the city is nine days off (day 18), the opening sixteen; the sentence's own second half ("the tournament's weeks had begun at that pole") wants "city". Fix 3.

### Scores, figures and counts — hold
Every bout with a score on the page was added. Regional: Lira's debut 26, the guild champion 27, Lira 25 / Karis 22, Ephram's title 26, Brom 15 and 23, 19/21/18, Cael 23, 22, 24, 22, 21, 23 (ch13's unrounded composites 23⅓, 22⅓, 24⅓, 21⅔, 21⅓, 23 and Seln's "three points"), 26 and 20 at the quarry town (26.00 and 20⅓), Lira 26/21, 25, Ephram 23/24, Karis 22/23, Brom 23/21, the confluence 21, 24, 23, 25, 22, Lira 27/25 and 26/25, Ephram 25/24 and 24/22, Brom 23/20; "thirteen exhibitions, thirteen won, twenty to twenty-six" in Vastin's digest (ch24:147) and "fifteen exhibition figures" in Daeva's file (ch49:85). Norhold, by the exchange: Lira 0–2, 2–1, 3–1, 3–0 = 8–4 and stopped (26/24); Karis 8–1 in three; Brom 9–0 and then 1–2, 2–1, 1–3, 3–0, 2–1 = 9–7 (24/23); Ephram 2–1, 1–1, 0–2, 3–0, 3–1 = 9–5; Lira 10–3 in eleven minutes; Ephram 1–3, 2–2, 1–3, 2–3 = 6–11; Zerin 0–3, 2–1, 1–2, 1–3 = 4–9 (29/27), the broadside's "nought to three, two to one, one to two, one to three" spelled for the ear; Brom's rematch 1–1, 2–1, 2–1, 3–0 = 8–3 (24/23); Marek 1–3, 3–2, 3–1, 3–0 = 10–6 (25/25); Ivenne 0–3, 3–1, 1–3, 3–1, 3–1 = 10–9 (25/24); the lattice-breaker 2–1, 1–3, 2–2, 0–3 = 5–9 (27/23); the captain 2–1 in four (30/27); the duelist 2–1 in four with no fire and no burst (28/25); the trial 2–2 and the ledger to Auremont; third place 3–1 with both ledgers; the match 1–0 on flags at the stoppage, seven of nine for the touch and four of nine, "No touch", for the strike. The bursts are counted on the page at every floor and agree with the per-day budgets the ledger fixes (six oak, five boards and planks, four slate and ring stone, about five clay): four at the mock ring, two on pine, four and one of five at the mill town, one, three of four on slate, four and three of five in the salt store, four of five on clay, four of four against the captain, none against the duelist or in the trials, nine in the match. Standings: second of six, first of five, first of six, first in the region "with a meet to spare", eleventh of fourteen to qualify, third of fourteen "as the count stands" and confirmed. Lira four regional titles and no bout lost until Zerin; Brom four and the plaque; Ephram one; Karis placed at every meet and shared a third; "I sent four of you onto three floors this week and I've got four of you back."

### Quotations against first occurrence — hold
Every line quoted later was checked where it was first said. Exact: "Pack for the third one. The halls at the third one are cold." (ch2 → ch15); Karis's roster sentence (ch2, said by Cael for her the same night); "Thank you for the form." / "Mm." (every Fifth-day); "Variance is cheap. Buy some." (ch13 → ch19 "Seln told me… to buy some variance"); "Nobody stands still" and "Walk" (ch15 → ch32, ch36); "Early." (ch27 → ch31, ch46, ch51, ch55, "Ready. Not early." ch46 → ch47, ch51); the four cards and "No answer recorded" (ch40 → ch51 "where card four lived"); Rooke's "I lose the bout… and we go to dinner" (ch40 → ch41); the captain's finding (ch41); "Scheduling is opinion." (ch32 → ch48); "It must be admitted" (ch23 → ch24 Vastin's smile); "Second time's the real rating" (ch36); Karis's card (ch53 "Air first. Not her. The leave runs out where the lane does." → ch54:145, ch55:49 exact); "she arrives third" and "*Permitted*" (ch53 → ch55); "Survive the first one." (ch54 → "One. Survived."); "going down well" (ch53 → ch56); Marek's page (ch51, read twice the same); Brom's Velmere line (ch39, protected); "Fifty-one minutes" (ch58 Brom, ch59 Lira, Book 4's Arbiter); the B4 minute's three rules in one consistent wording (ch53:181, ch56:49–57, ch59:93); Hesk's clock "an hour fast" (ch13, ch27, ch33, ch44, ch52, ch53, ch54); "slept like a quarry stone" counted aloud ("twice since the bluff", ch28, ch45); "Things adults say that the paper does not support" (ch44); "Standardization is legibility" (ch34, Book 3's line); Lira's "Huh. It's small." and "You mean it's small" (ch15, the B4 echo). The one quotation seam is small: Rooke's review at the wool town, "I'll lend you that exchange at this meet" (ch9:257), said when the meet is over; "this time" is what he means. Fix 2.

### Facts, names and numbers — three small families
- **Brom's wrist.** ch14:145: "He began to strap his right wrist out of habit, though it had been healed for a month." The knuckles were B4's final, five months and a recess before; ch6:149 already has "the knuckles had healed, but he strapped them now from habit". Fix 1.
- **The colour-guard at the opening.** ch31:185 "four men in grey on either side of a tall staff" reads as eight; the ledger has the banner "borne by four colour-guards, alone", and the closing and the standings have a guard of two (ch51:225, ch59:155). Fix 4.
- **Seln's cases.** ch53:15–35 has him open "his own case" for the grey offcuts, put "the knife and the rule into the case", and take "the case" up to his room, on the eve of finals; ch58:115 then says "The locked case stood under the table by his chair… He had not opened it. He did not open it now." The ledger's open-thread line is "Seln's locked case: carried and unopened all book", and Book 4's small case holds the cipher page. A reader who has kept that thread reads ch53 as the case being opened. Fixes 6–9 give the eve a pen-case.

Smaller, for the ledger and not fixed: Cael's year's ledger (ch60:5) calls the span "near enough a year" by "nobody's calendar but his own" (the day-count makes it about thirty-seven weeks); Withrow's eighteen years of chancellorship, Vastin's nineteen in his room and Ephram's two at the column's head are new; the Tide anomaly is touched three times with the three words (ch1, ch45, ch60), the entry inventory being the third, against the map's "two"; Rooke's "twenty years" is said six times; the two Hesk documents of the sixteenth (the pine-case note and "Sixteen suits you") are kept distinct (ch4:213) and match B4 ch37 and ch62; ch53's "given him a piece of the next day in place of a present" is B4 ch53's own log line ("Nobody gave me a present tonight…"), correctly placed the night before the evaluation; ch44's "Three people had decided that the day mattered" is B4 ch37's four places under the good lamp.

### Seams — Book 4 into ch1, ch60 into Book 6
From Book 4: sixteen; six confirmed and the anomaly; six free on the sprung oak and the seventh billed to the morning; the landing beat unshortened; "Rent from waking, every day, spent or not" (B4's word, restored in r1); Pressure flat at every plate, four blows a sitting in the right shoulder; the read eleven of twelve at the fourth weight; Compression "Nobody official has ever seen it. Two years."; Ember four instants on the copper pot, two contacts a bout "our rule, not theirs"; the record holds five, two public, a thin third shown; one stamp renewed in Bracken's upright hand; the recess counter sheet with one name; "Gratitude is the leak"; the named reading room and its card (ch26:5); the clause banked, sixth part, fourth subsection; the crown on the shelf over the settle (ch3:169); the Arbiter's fifty-one minutes; Brom third in the cohort, nineteen exercises round three four a week, "until somebody filed for him"; Ephram's sheet signed on d5; Withrow's "eighteen"; Bracken twelve years and the mirror; Hesk's "Sixteen suits you" and the pine case; the Archmarshal's eleven days and the file note as belief; Ilsev's second referral pending; Havel's four; the watchers two and two on the bell with the recess's one dated change in a single Log line (ch1:97, as #37 O7 allows). Nothing from Book 4 is contradicted. Lira's letter to Fiske is sent (ch1:77) and never answered or mentioned again (see §4).

Into Book 6: seventeen (staged on the page at the rest day, ch43–45, no date printed); seven confirmed, the seventh Storm-adjacent by the notice verbatim, its stability flag standing at the close ("The flag stands.", ch60:139); the anomaly uncounted; the bank spent permanently and listed (ch60:11–19); Shadow never deployed and Seln never told ("The debt it was sealed for is still owed and still unspoken"); the costed rule initialled by four (ch59:103–107); the match record verbatim and "the most-reviewed result" (the copy line, Auremont in it); every public theory wrong and the words never used outside the circle; third of fourteen; Brom continental Copper champion with the summons collected on road day nine, "ninety days", "The recess's problem." (ch60:55); Lira "Silver bracket. Next cycle." (ch60:75); Karis's quarterfinal over Ternhall's best; Ephram the round of sixteen and the caller; Seln at the middle of the bench from the first waystation with "records and floor scheduling" against his name on his own manifest (ch60:87–97), one true report with "Routine." left off (ch58:103–109), the case unopened; Vastin's warning off the record, his testimony verbatim and set aside, "He declined to discuss the Level 4 designation" rendered as "I won't answer that one" (ch58:213); Ilsev's return verbatim and her first private sheet; Havel's fifth; the question entered for formal proceedings, no vote, the flag "no living hand had authorized" and no age; Daeva's "Rematch." and the banner to Auremont; the scouting economy closed benign (ch17) and the dossier in circulation; Fenmark closed as a wound at the coat-rack (ch15) and shut for good by Lira's "scrapyard" at the closing (ch59:147); Reydan's one line; Vell's letter; Hesk's letters; the watchers back on the twelfth day (ch60:167); the old volume's last line on the last ferry and the road's entries in it, the year's ledger in Hesk's book (C6). Hand-off items for the Book 6 reconciliation, not for this book: no Norhold session was drafted (C8), so two renewals stand on paper at the close and A11's "four stamps" goes to the owner as the plan says; "two watchers and a horse" at a market-town bridge is the shape Book 6 opens on; Withrow's eighteen years and Vastin's nineteen are now in the edition.

## 2. Knowledge boundaries — hold

Only Lira, Brom and Karis know the mechanism, and the book says so in the place it must (ch44:127 "Four of them knew both his ledgers… Two of them had chosen… not to ask", C5's fix). Seln infers management from four items he never files (ch17:93), reads the commercial file once and copies nothing, notes the two ignitions a round "where nobody was looking" and "did not ask himself why" (ch26:119–121), writes "Variance is cheap" as arithmetic, sits at the blue door, writes the second slip in a nobody's hand and squares it, is outside the council that seals Shadow and is never told why, writes the one report that needs no building and leaves off "Routine.", and sits in the middle of the bench; no sentence states his reason, and his one story at the blue door has no "I" in it (ch44:187). Ephram is kept outside the back room, "was not of the circle and knew it" (ch58:79), and his handshake says "Whatever you are" (ch46). Rooke's nearest approach is the tap on the buttoned pocket (ch40:91–95) and "I'd thought I'd mind not knowing what it is. I find I don't." (ch53:59). Vastin writes "Everything I saw today, I already had a word for" (ch43:55), does not know what the boy is and does not write that down either (ch43:77), evaluates "how you went about it" and says "The method is not a boy's" (ch58:233), and goes to the convening with nothing of fragments, of Seln, or of makers. Daeva buys the file and "did not try to work out from the file what he was" (ch49:91), knows the lane was hers and "did not try to work out how he had done it" (ch57:25), and her vocabulary stays at "mine", "a piece", "something I couldn't name". Umber's furthest word is "the schedule met something it doesn't contain" (ch57:139). The mechanism words appear only inside the notice and Cael's own log ("digestion" once, ch57:223; "witnessed" only of the Standard's sealing, ch5:175, ch31:47). The Architect plants sit at source altitude and go no higher: "A note for the cold term" (ch6:257), the slip unopened in the founding articles (ch26:43–55), "The strata keep holding me up" (ch23:223), the steward's bricked-up doorway and "cheaper than deciding" (ch30:223), "Twice is starting to look like a door somebody built on purpose. A note for after Norhold." (ch32:263), "Again." (ch45:87), the sigil "older than the city… and older than the law" in Daeva's own perception (ch49:113), Umber's "It was built for a world with a schedule in it" (ch57:139), and the convening's "a flag no living hand had authorized" with the trace "closed" in a sub-layer (ch60:125). No "four centuries", no "[UNBOUND]", no maker, no reset. The still place is touched only in the three inventories with its three words, and no Tide practitioner appears. The consent boundary and the minute hold: nothing is manufactured (ch56:51 counts the whole sitting), she begins it alone, the wish is on paper the night before (protected 28), and the gap in rule three is named on the page before it is crossed and repaired in the rule afterward (ch56:59–63, ch59:93–103).

## 3. Book-wide repetition — the worst ten

Counts are `grep -oiE` over all 60 chapters (hits / chapters). "Keep" names instances that earn their place.

1. **The squared page** — "square / squared" 198 / 51; "flat" 186 / 58. Seln squares a file to the counter's edge, Bracken squares a folder, Karis squares a stack, Cael squares the card to the desk, Vastin squares the slip to the blotter, a note is squared at the corners, a strip is squared between two papers, "her small square hand", "his square capitals", "square to the edge", "both hands flat on the table", "palms flat", "a flat voice", "flat palm". It is this book's "eleven". Keep it where it is a character's signature and the scene is about paper: Bracken's sand (ch2), Seln's strip (ch53:27), Vastin's slip (ch5), the manifest (ch60). Target: one squaring and one "flat" per chapter.
2. **The superlative-ever** — "the X-est thing (he|anybody) had ever (seen|heard|done)" 41 / 29: "the clearest touch Cael had ever seen", "the most careful thing anybody had ever done to him on a floor", "the bravest thing I've heard on this floor", "the best piece of ordinary work he had seen anybody do all sitting", "the most restful price anybody had put on him in a year", "the most dangerous sentence anybody had spoken to Cael since he came to Norhold". When every scene has one, none does. Keep five: the clearest touch (ch8), the bravest (ch8, Ephram), Seln's one long second (ch17), Karis's "worst moment of her life" (ch56), Daeva's "never in her life been so happy about anything she did not understand" (ch57).
3. **"as he read / did / considered everything"** — 27 / 18 (ch13 ×2, ch19, ch23, ch25, ch26 ×2, ch29 ×2, ch36 ×2, ch37 ×2, ch43 ×2, ch44 ×2, ch45, ch48, ch53, ch58 ×2…), and its cousin "honestly, as he considered everything" (Brom, four times). It has become the author's way of saying "characteristically". Cut to the first for each character.
4. **The measured pause and the silence** — "for a moment / for a long moment" 120 / 56; "for a while" 72; "for a long time / some time" 82; "Nobody said anything" 31 / 26; "said nothing / did not say anything" 92 / 48; "Nobody spoke" 9. Nearly every exchange of dialogue has one. Keep them in the hour (ch58) and the panel night (ch57), where length is the point.
5. **The micro-timing coinage** — "a fraction" 53 / 24, "half a beat" 36 / 19, "quarter-beat / a quarter of a beat" 35 / 16, "a hand's breadth" 18, "a handspan" 6, "a stride" 119 / 33. The fight language has to be exact, and "half a beat" is the book's own unit (the read's margin, the card's "Half"); but "a fraction early/late" is the read's gloss repeated at nearly every bout, and "a stride" has become the only word for distance. Thin "a fraction" to one per bout; let "a stride" share with "a pace", "a step" and plain feet.
6. **Eleven** — 68 / 37. Canon stays: eleven names on the roster, eleven of twelve at the fourth weight, eleven days, eleven questions, eleven minutes (the rating interval, seventeen times and the title of ch33), eleven relocations and the captain's "Nobody ever gives us eleven", the eleven words of the entry, eleventh of fourteen, the eleventh bell. Reached for: "eleven more times, by Karis's count" (ch3:195), "eleven of them by the heel alone" (ch4:201), "Eleven pages" (the clerk's notebook ch18:189 and Zerin's file ch34:237, ch49:81 — two different documents the same length), "in eleven breaths" (ch25:231), "seven, eight; perhaps eleven" halls (ch28:149), "Eleven exchanges" (ch33:109, :259), "eleven years ago… the drains" (ch24:173), "eleven days by coach" (ch44:183), "the eleven paces at its middle" (ch51:9), "eleven of them in the lock-house" with Seln's "Eleven" as the road's last joke (ch52:23, ch60:103–105), "There were eleven more in that" (ch53:51–53), "eleven seconds by a carter's count" (ch56:109), "There were eleven of them now" (ch60:135). The road joke needs the verses to stay eleven; the rest can go to other numbers so that the elevens that matter ring.
7. **The handshake aphorism and the sound of the crowd.** Every exhibition ends with the opponent delivering a finding at the handshake ("Story undersold you", "You fight like an auditor", "You found the change of guard… charged me toll", "Then I hope it's cargo", "we found the edges", "He's better than the woodcuts"), and nearly every bout is scored by a crowd simile ("the sound a crowd makes when it has just been told the shape of something", "like a dropped tray", "when a juggler drops nothing", "like a crowd on a quay watching a man go into the water", "like men at a graveside", "the sound a market makes when the bell has gone for closing"). Each is good; together they are a formula. The aphorisms are mostly canon or protected; thin the crowd similes to one a bout and let some bouts end on the flag.
8. **"to nobody in particular / to nobody"** 25 / 23; "nodded once" 14 / 12; "very still" 22 / 20; "which from Rooke/Seln/Brom was a speech/approval/a remark" 8 / 8; "He found that he…" 35 / 29; "That was all." / "That was the whole of it." 29 / 24; "like a man who" 11. The scene-ending beat and the character-gloss, as in Book 4.
9. **Ephram's ears** — red, pink, scarlet, "all the way round": 13 / 11, eleven of them Ephram's (ch13, ch18, ch21, ch25, ch28, ch32, ch34, ch46…), plus the Shield reserve, the delegation head and a stout man with a pie. It is a running joke the book has earned, but by the trial it fires on every scene he is in. Keep the first (ch13), the trial-caller appointment (ch25) and the handshake; drop the rest or vary the sign.
10. **Repeated images.** "rain on a roof" three times (the Rhagen block writing ch40:221, documents read aloud ch50:69, the clocks ch58:135); a stone into a pond / stones into a lake / circles from a stone / falling into a pond (ch30, ch40, ch56 ×2); "like a dog that…" four; "as a buyer looks at a horse" (ch35) and "a crowd greeting a horse it has backed" (ch11); the "checked the sum twice and did not like it" nod (ch41:269, ch58:85, ch59:133); "a wave breaking" (ch31 ×2, ch59 ×2); "the way a crowd steps aside for a steward" (ch53 and ch56 — deliberate, keep); "struck flint" (motif, keep); the rocking table and the pump (ch33, ch34, ch41, ch48, ch53, ch58, ch59 — the back room's furniture named at every visit); "walked his circle" 14 (motif, keep). Also the Log frame: "He wrote it that night / at the window" 32 / 26 and "wrote it down" 62 / 40, as in Book 4 — the entries are right; vary the sentence that introduces them.

None of these is fixed below; every one needs a reading pass and not a find-and-replace.

## 4. Arc and pacing

**Where it sags.** The confluence, ch21–23. Five exhibitions across two chapters, each with its own lesson: the merry Blade (item two), the caravan captain (the clock), the eastern Stone (a paragraph), the coastal Wind (a paragraph) and the barge-master (the bed). The captain and the barge-master are the two the book needs — the one to set up Rhagen's "no clock" and the other to teach "the man underneath the shapes" beside Lira's builder — and both are good; but the Blade's point and the two short ones are the third and fourth time the shape "walk the floor, find the thing, charge for it, handshake, Log with the bill" has run in two meets, and ch21's and ch23's Log entries restate in Cael's hand what the bout has just shown (the panes and the clock, ch21:359–367; the bed, ch23:179–185). Lose five to seven hundred words across the two chapters: the Blade to a paragraph, both Logs to their bill lines. The same economy can be taken at the mill town (ch12: the two academy Irons are already a paragraph; the curiosity Bronze's nine questions earn their room) and at the quarry town, where ch18's audit (ch18:207–245) re-lists what ch15–17 have shown the market to hold. The second soft place is the empty board, ch47, which is thin by design (3,515 words) and reads so: the register's hour in the rain is the chapter's one image and it is a good one; the fishwife's "somebody's got to come down" is the plant; the medium session is the business. I would not cut it but would not add to it. The one Norhold set piece a reader could do with less of is the third-place trial (ch51), whose third and fourth exchanges are summarised already and whose second is the chapter; it is right that the book makes the found family win something on the page, and "The argument and the table" pays Marek, Karis and Brom in one gesture; keep, trim the first exchange's recap of the semifinal's shapes.

**Where it rushes.** Nowhere that costs the reader. The morning card is heard through a stone ceiling (ch54:35–39), which is the right choice; Rhagen's semifinal loss is one line by ruling; the trial final is Ephram's ear against a wall. Brom's summons at the district seat is four sentences on a tailboard and is better for it. The two hinges a reader may want slower are the deferral (ch33:59–73), which puts the ring back a week in a single notice, and the convening, which is a window and stays one.

**The qualifying season.** It does not sag as a whole. M1 is the book's best first third since Book 4's: the clause, the mirror, the card, the map room, the mock ring and page thirty-one teach the instrument and the doctrine's price together, and Withrow's order of operations (ch4:125–133) is the thesis the closing pays. M2's two meets are distinct (pine and the wall; the crane-beam's light and the pillar corner). M3 at the quarry town is the season's richest movement: the five-day silence, the coat-rack, page three as a funnel, the service side, the foreman's bill, the quay steps. What a reader feels by the confluence is not sag but the accumulation of one shape; the fix is economy, not restructuring.

**At Norhold.** Thirty-four chapters are on the road to, in, or on the road from the city, as the map planned, and the city is built well enough to hold them: the Concourse from the rise, the keeper counting on the stair, the likenesses, the sockets, the west tower's lamp, the betting rows' frame, the cookshop's sign, the lock-keeper's bell, the copy line, the bedsheets with HALCENVANE on them. The length a reader feels is in the returns to the same furniture (the back room's rocking table and the pump, the boards at dusk, the long table's council) rather than in any one scene. M6's three floors are the book's middle at its best, and the book's finest quiet writing is Karis's "Unknown." (ch34), Lira's "Not glad tonight. Morning." (ch26) and the walk with two hours and a line written on a hand (ch36).

**Plants and payments.** Paid in-book: Gault's handle (ch2 → ch6, ch13, ch20, ch21, ch24, ch27, ch52 the ribbon, ch59 the box held shut); "Pack for the third one" (ch2 → ch15); Hesk's "pack wool" (ch4 → ch15); Karis's strip in Rooke's manual (ch6 → ch12); the pine floors by the wall (ch5 → ch7, ch9); the buried Copper (ch3 → ch8 → ch36 "Second time's the real rating"); the second-year entry (ch3 → ch11 the late half-turn → ch22 the plain entry saved → ch32); the pillar corner (ch10 → ch11 → ch21 "That's mine"); page thirty-one (ch6 → ch12 → ch13); "Variance is cheap" (ch13 → ch19); Lira's tell (ch11 → ch27 the hidden gather); the corner game's "Early" (ch27 → ch31, ch46, ch55); the Shield clock (ch21 → ch25 → ch33 → ch40 "He had solved a clock"); Zerin's missing gather (ch25 → ch33 "She doesn't gather" → ch35); "Behind" (ch33 → ch35 → ch36 "You did it twice"); Marek's joins (ch19 → ch25 the door → ch37 the sixth gap and the cheap gate); Ternhall's third page (ch34 → ch38 the unfinished lattice → ch39 "we taught her they were parts"); Rooke's card four (ch40 → ch41 the tap → ch51, ch54); the diagonal (ch45:245 → ch46 → ch47 "A thing you've only written down is a thing you've found too late"); the read reaching the air (ch46 "Half." → ch47 → ch50 the squall → ch52 ten in ten and Brom unheard → ch55 the woman or the air); the fishwife's "somebody's got to come down" (ch47 → ch48); seat twelve (ch29 → ch32 → ch42 → ch43 "Somebody announced it for him" → ch54); the two initials (ch5 → ch24 → ch29 → ch50, carried open on purpose); the strata note (ch6 → ch26 → ch32 → ch45); the slip-and-stove (B4 → ch53 "There had been one before. Now there were two."); Brom's "going down well" (ch53 → ch56); "Fifty-one minutes" (B4 → ch58 → ch59); the lock-keeper's verses (ch44 → ch52 → ch60 "Eleven"); the quarry-stone sleep (ch28 → ch45 ×2); the crooked frame (ch24 → ch26 the roster pinned under it); the masts humming (ch50 → ch55 → ch56); the cap seam (ch52 → ch54:161–165); "I'll have changed it by the day after tomorrow" (ch52 → ch54:127–129); Daeva's coast exercise (ch49 → ch57 "two ways to use it"); the reserve's small plant (ch25:281–287 → not paid, and rightly: "the block ran out before he had"). Carried to Book 6 on purpose: the rematch, the people who want this to stop, Brom's review, the watchers' return, Vastin's "I have stopped meaning to ask", Ilsev's sheet, the two initials, the strata note, Reydan's one line, Vell's session, the Fenmark table-keeper. One thread fades rather than pays: Lira's letter to Fiske, posted in the recess's sixth week and unanswered (ch1:77–81), never mentioned again, though Fiske said "I'll be watching the Iron bracket"; a reader does not miss it until the road home, and one clause there would close it. The "Somebody ought to file for her" line for the buried Copper (ch8, ch36) is left open beside Brom's own review, which reads as intended.

**Development cadence.** About two realisation beats a chapter, as planned, with the bump in M6–M7 ("Silver is not a bigger Iron", "the doctrine is filling", "the first one I didn't set", the ladder), and the progression vocabulary front-loaded as §7.2 asks: tier, rank, rating, par, band, seeding, exhibition, validation ("adjacent"), the three words, the strike, the composite, the documented rate, all taught by ch13 and surfacing afterward at the pin (page thirty-one), the threshold, Brom's review, the trial rule, the bank, the entry, the ledger. The supporting cast all get their arcs without taking the budget: Lira from "waiting for the bill" to "It's a better want. It costs more." to "Put her down" to "Silver bracket. Next cycle."; Brom priced right by himself first (the buried Copper, the funnel, "Sound", the hill fighter's five, Marek's "I read it as your mistake", the wall, the step, "The recess's problem"); Karis from the clause to the trade in records to "Unknown." to the burned plan to the finding about Cael's loneliness (ch39:117, the book's most generous page) to the model to "Now it's the house's"; Ephram from the second weight to freight to trial caller to "pre-sold" to "South." to the handshake to the plain version, his ears a running line; Seln from the last line of the roster to the middle of the bench by one table at a blue door and one word on a tailboard; Rooke from the near-question to the pocket to the pads to "household" to the mercy line; Withrow's order of operations, her sentence at a waystation, her toast, "we taught her they were parts", her absence from the board and her reading of a letter's outside; Gault from the handle to fourth on the figures to the ribbon; Bracken's mirror, pouch, quarry stone and the herald's fine; Umber from the seals to the screen to "approve it before somebody makes me invent something" to his own boot on the floor to "under"; Vastin's five stages exactly as §3 D asks; Ilsev's pen stopping and her girlhood hand; Havel packing the notebook like part of the work; and Daeva, the best new character the edition has made, whose window (ch49) is the book's finest cutaway and whose "Am I early, or are you late?", "Specimen.", "Today somebody asked me something." and the two ways to use a slackened lane are all hers.

