editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:235:The court was dark, and the fountain went on in its basin below. Beside him on the sill the copy lay where he had put it, first page face up to the night, *Given under the Compact's hand* at its foot in type no larger than the rest. He did not close it. He left it there, open, waiting for better light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:245:He stood in the middle of it and understood the woman on the Ostrand road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:179:"*Given under the Compact's hand.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:255:"It is exactly what it says it is. There is nothing behind it at all." He said it in his mild voice, as he might have reported a floor schedule. "In my trade a man is taught four grades of pressure, in their order. The record. The instrument. The knock, which is paper nobody will own. And last the hand, which is a road with no paper at all. I was taught that the hand was the worst of them, and I believed it for fifteen years." He shook his head, very slightly. "I had the order wrong. What stood in the road was a glove." He looked down at the last line of the leaf. "*Given under the Compact's hand.* That is the only true sentence of its kind I have been shown this year. This is the hand. It does not have another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:146:FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:201:*To the Chancellor of the chartered academy at Halcenvane, and to the enrollee of record therein named. The registry, in support of the proceedings presently convened at the seat at Ostrand, directs the attendance of the said enrollee at the registry facility at the district seat for pre-evaluation assessment, transport and escort provided, effective on presentation. The academy is directed to produce the enrollee to the bearers of this order. Given under the Compact's hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:271:"Your files are in order," said Withrow. "All four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:293:"Doors open both ways," said Rooke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:87:*Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:185:*Eighteen. A man's tools should fit the road he's actually on, not the one they promised him. Your grandmother would say I'm being unsubtle. She'd be right. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:229:*Wind-adjacent — Lira, the burst. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere, the push. Iron-adjacent — Brom, the read, which found the fourteen. Compression-adjacent — Reydan, the give. Ember-adjacent — Karis, the spark, two contacts and no more. Shadow-adjacent — the stair, sealed, rent from waking. Storm-adjacent — Daeva, the lane; moved twice this week, a quarter hour and then forty minutes, and settled. Anchor-adjacent — the hand with no face; idle by naming; wants doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:233:*Still open. Still real. Patience.*
