editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:315:He did not take a slip of paper. He did not open the letter-book of fair copies in which everything that left the room had been entered for nineteen years. He did not open the lowest drawer, where the folder with its tab lay under his own hand, *Pending a proceeding worth sending it to*. He knew now that there was no proceeding, and never would be, and that the tab would stay as it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:161:"How many boxes is your research?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:243:"I resigned fifteen years ago. I just never filed it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:79:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:121:"It's not a question. It's a corridor. Every door in it is real law, and every door opens one way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:91:*The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:153:"They benched their best instrument because he measures true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:193:*They filed. After the hearing I won, and the inspection, and a tournament that ran out of numbers for me, they've gone back to the shelf for one more way to ask. Fine. Their text has lost to ours before. Karis has the code open already. Note the date. The season's open.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:240:"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:264:*Nine. Taken in earnest from a friend who asked me to show him straight — so I showed him straight, and the architecture did what it does with earnest, and the notice said "non-hostile," which it has never said, because there has never been a fight like it in the log. Ephram asked for the true measurement. He got it. He doesn't know he gave me a piece of himself for it; he consented to a fight, not to the thing the fight fed. I wrote the price down before the bout, the way the covenant says, so that when it cost — and it cost — I'd have written the cost before I collected it, which is the only line between me and what the office did at a road cut and in a records hall — spending people whose names it never bothered to write down, and asking nobody anything.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:304:Seln had looked round a reading room at the top of a library once, on the evening of a birthday, with six people in it and a honey cake. *It is a good room*, he had said. *I have been in a great many.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:39:"Is good law." She looked at him across the desk. "I don't say that as your chancellor. Before you ever came up our road I had the Greyvane transcript sent from Ostrand, and I went through it with every tool twenty years at the registry gave me, the way I used to go through other people's appeals when I was paid to find what was wrong with them. I came away with nothing. Their paper is the same. Honest work, both of them, and nothing in either to find." She set both hands on the blotter, a hand's width apart. "That's two true things, and they point opposite ways, and I can only stand on one of them. So I've decided which. I'm going to stand on the first, out loud, in front of that seat and the broadsides and every inspector who ever opens that folder, with this whole house on my back, knowing where the second one goes." She did not lower her eyes. "Make a note of the word *choosing*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:343:"I have nine things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no enrollment, no protection — and four people who signed out of the world that was supposed to provide them, and one of the nine taken from a hand the world sent to hold me, and one taken in earnest from a friend, and the machine's own maintenance records in a case under that canvas, which we can't file and can't publish and are carrying east anyway because the truth is worth carrying even when it can't be spent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:347:"Let them escalate," said Lira. She had her feet to the fire and her chin on her knees. "They'll need better maps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:349:"And better roads," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:357:"Noted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:79:*None, Advocate.* He had said so in the fourth sitting, in so many words, and Cael had written it down and had gone on bringing receipts.

