79:He read it in the flattest voice the hall had ever held — the voice, Cael thought, that he had used to certify enrollment records forty times a year for thirty years, the boilerplate voice, the voice of law nobody had needed yet. "Registry code, evaluation procedures, chapter fourteen. *Where an evaluation conducted under standard Arbiter protocol returns no valid classification, the following shall apply in sequence.* One. *Nullification of any enrollment, sponsorship, or standing predicated upon the evaluand's practitioner status.* Two. *Suspension of practitioner status pending review, such review to be initiated by the registry at its discretion.* Three." He paused, and the pause was not for effect; it was a registrar reaching for a term he had never once had to say aloud. "*Asset-restriction.* Section fourteen, part nine. *The registry may restrict, freeze, or hold in abeyance any standing, record, sponsorship, endorsement, travel credential, or institutional relationship attaching to an unclassified person, pending resolution of practitioner status.*" He closed the volume. "That is the procedure for an evaluation that returns no valid classification. It's codified. It has been codified since before I was born. I have never once seen it executed, because in thirty years I have never once seen an evaluation return nothing."
107:"So here is what it is. It is unpassable — and I want to be precise about the word, because precision is the only defense we have left tonight. Not *rigged*. Rigging requires a rigger — an assessor bought, a frame tampered with, a finding falsified — and this instrument requires none of that and contains none of it. Every clause in it is real law. Every step is a real procedure. It is unpassable *by design without a designer*: the protocol simply has no bin for him, and the procedure for no-bin is written down, so the outcome was determined the moment the petition was filed, before the first session sits, and every honest official who touches it from here to the ruling can act with total integrity and the result will not change by a hair." She looked around the table. "It's not a question. It's a corridor. Every door in it is real law, and every door opens one way."
117:"There's something else in it I can't place." She didn't look up. "The assay clause worked for three years because it bypasses Arbiter evaluation entirely. Evaluation by demonstration — a floor, a panel, witnesses, a record. No Arbiter in the loop. That's *why* it could hold him: it never asked the instrument the question the instrument can't answer." Her pen stopped. "And this petition's one non-negotiable demand is that he stand in front of an Arbiter. They've conceded the provision, conceded the record, conceded everything — and the one thing they will not concede is the *stratum*. They're not trying to beat the provision. They're trying to move him back inside the instrument." She looked at the page for a moment longer. "I don't know why that distinction feels important. Noting it."
145:"Every time the *people* failed," Lira said. She said it carefully, the drill-book still open on her knee, a woman testing a floor's footing before committing weight. "That's your sentence. You've said it since Greyvane. The text holds when the people fail." She looked at the embers. "What if the people didn't fail this time? What if they read the text better than we did and wrote a new one?"
153:*I can work within the system. I have the receipts: a hearing, a provision, a stamp renewed four times, a tournament their own clause put me in. The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed. This is more text. We'll read it better than they do. We always have.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:73:The district seat's advancement office posted the term's review schedule to Bracken's counter, and Bracken walked the relevant sheet up to the residence himself — which he did not do for forms; he did it for things — and laid it on the common table where the delegation was eating, and pointed at the line without comment, because the line did its own work. *Advancement Review, mandatory, per continental title: Brom, Halcenvane, Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier formal. Docketed: the last Fifth-day of term, district seat, Ostrand. Panel: standard.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:75:"Standard panel," Bracken said. "Which means two assessors and a sigil frame, the same room your friend stood in." He nodded at Lira. "The title triggers the review inside ninety days of the award; the academy applied to move it, and the district re-docketed it to the last Fifth-day of term, so you sit it after a term of work instead of in the middle of one. The season record supplies the evidence. I have processed eleven of these in thirty years and every one of them found for advancement, because a continental title is not a thing the registry can price below the tier it was won at without the registry looking foolish, and the registry does not look foolish. Everybody in this office is saying the word *formality* quietly." He looked at Brom, who was looking at the sheet. "I'll say it to your face, because you are a man who keeps records: it is a formality. Iron-tier by the last Fifth-day of term. Your family's ledger closes."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:145:*Be it known that the Guilds Compact, by its registry, having convened formal proceedings upon the question of the classification designated [SHATTERED] and its bearing upon evaluation integrity within chartered institutions, hereby files a formal challenge to enrollment basis against the said enrollment.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:147:*The challenge does not allege fraud in the enrollment's execution, nor contest the text of the provision under which it was executed. It asserts that an unclassifiable practitioner's presence within a chartered institution's evaluation architecture presents an evaluation-integrity question which the demonstration provision was not drafted to answer and cannot answer, and petitions the registry for compelled re-evaluation of the enrollee under standard Arbiter protocol, the enrollment to stand or fall upon that evaluation's result.*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-01.md:149:*Proceedings to be convened at the registry seat at Ostrand upon a term-length schedule. The respondent institution and the enrollee of record are directed to answer within fourteen days.*

