editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:Gault sat at the trestle in the middle of the north side with his case beside him on the bench. It was the case with the handle, the one he carried his instruments in, and he had set it down very straight, as he always did, as if it might have opinions about the bench. On his right sat the Ash instructor with her book held against her chest. On his left sat the Mire instructor, a square calm woman with a pencil behind each ear. Each of the three had a card in front of them, ruled into three columns, execution, control and effect, and the wing added the three cards with no strike, which was the house's own way and not the tournaments'. The desk clerk from the counter sat at the end of the trestle with the wing's book open, to write the figure down when there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:111:Then he took his place inside the rate. That was how he thought of it now, since Norhold: a room inside the room. The house had a record of him, and the record held what it held. On sprung oak the house had seen six bursts in a bout; on these boards that meant four, and never a fifth. Karis's spark to two contacts in a bout and no more. Reydan's give kept shut, so that a blow which landed on his forearm went on up the arm into the shoulder, as it would in any honest Iron. The push no more than a flat hand. And the rest of it, the lane and the weight behind it, not anywhere at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:147:The Shield had set his plane square in front of him to turn a straight blow, and he had his whole weight in it, the way a man leans into a door to hold it shut. It was a good guard against anything that came at it from the front. It had nothing at all to say about something that came from the side. A push at a fighting load, the thing Cael had given a Gold five times in one sitting, would have taken that braced plane on its edge and turned the whole man round on his own planted foot like a gate swinging on a hinge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:149:He did not give it. He gave a flat hand, at the edge of the plane, the way any careful Iron might test a guard, and the plane turned it off with a soft sound like a palm on a drum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:205:There were columns ruled down the page, and every line of every column was a bout. The first line was the first figure the house had put on him in the qualifying year, in the long room with five chairs and a rope. After it came every floor they had fought on, in every town, and the figures went down the page in Ephram's square, cramped hand, with the floor and the town and the opponent's Path beside each. Twenty-three, twenty, twenty-six, twenty-two, twenty-four, twenty-one, twenty-five, twenty-three. In the margins there were notes in smaller writing. *Soft boards. Loud gallery. He let the Blade have the first.* And once, against a figure of twenty, *bad fish at breakfast (mine, not his)*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:45:He did not burst. He stepped the way an honest Iron steps against a Current, a fraction late, and met the end of her curve with his forearm up. Her strike came off the bend and took him across the forearm, flat and fast, and the give was there, Reydan's give, waiting to drink the whole of the blow into the arm and keep it. He shut it. He let the blow go on up the arm the way it would have gone in anybody, into the elbow and then the shoulder, and felt it arrive there like a door slammed somewhere in a house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:121:He was on one knee on the bottom step, threading a chain through the stair's rail. It was a long chain, the kind the porters used on the cart-shed doors, and he had already run it twice round the newel post. As the four of them came up he drew the last link through, set a padlock in it, and snapped the padlock shut. Then he stood up and hung a card on the chain, square in the middle, where it would be read by anybody who came to the stair. The card was in his square capitals. *PROGRAM FLOOR CLOSED TO OBSERVERS. BY ORDER.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:Gault had added the three cards in his head, and again on the back of his hand. *Twenty-two and two-thirds. Entered.* The clerk had written it in the wing's book. Up in the gallery four pencils had written it too, and Cael had heard them. Ephram had come down the room from the east rail with the soft-folded chart and entered it below the Blade, in his cramped square hand, *gave her the first, bad arm*, and had not drawn a question mark beside it. Then he had looked up past Cael's shoulder at the man from Rhagen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:193:It was at the foot of a short covering note, in brown ink, and it was the Chief Adjudicator's own, written large and a little unsteadily, by a hand that had shaken once at a window in the west tower and evidently still did. Karis had the note under her palm and the rest of the courtesy copy squared beside it: the adjudication office's whole file from Norhold, the match's record, the nine panel charts, and on a sheet by itself the entry Cael had last seen hanging on a nail by the north tunnel. *Result: stoppage, Daeva, fourth exchange. Performance rating: unscorable under standard. Referred.*
