editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:81:He put his right palm flat against the point at his breastbone, where his chest had found it, and leaned, and then did not lean. He gave it the push at fighting load, the thing he had taken from the Bronze at Ardenmere, the thing he had given a Gold five times in one sitting at Norhold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:91:Four pushes and she had not taken a step. Four pushes and he was still in the cut, with three points round him and the woman on her flat patch of crown, and every push had moved nobody but him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:111:She was at the edge of the north ditch, where the camber fell away into it, sitting in the chalk with one leg out in front of her and her hands flat behind her. She had spent four bursts. He had not seen the third or the fourth, and he did not know which had put her there. He knew only that her right foot was turned at an angle no foot was meant to hold, and that she was holding very still. One of the four stood over her, a yard off, with his stave across his body in both hands. He did not touch her. He did not need to. He stood between her and the coach and looked at her, and she looked back at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:115:At the near horse, Karis had her right hand flat in the air a hand's breadth above the animal's foreleg, as if she were warming it at a fire.
