editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:29:Brom took his hand away and said nothing about it. He looked at Cael for the space of a breath, with the look he gave a floor before he stood on it, and then went to the far end of the painted lines and began unrolling the wraps for his forearms. It was all the comment Brom would ever make, and Cael felt it settle on him like a coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:219:He had it by heart. Every notice he had ever had he knew by heart, because each of them had found him somewhere with nothing else to look at, a yard, a stair, a shut room, and the one from Norhold on one knee on a stone floor, with a healer holding his hand. *Note: pressure-differential component; corridor-seeding component. Short range.* At the time it had read like a clerk's description of somebody else's coat. Tonight he thought he could put his hand on each line of it. The lane was the corridor; he seeded it, and it grew where he put it, and stayed while he left it alone. The tilt of the air, the ears, the dust running downhill, that was the pressure. Short range was fifteen feet. And the notice had said nothing at all about the read going into the air and leaving the body behind, or about ten counts of everything slow. Notices never told you what a thing cost. They only told you what it was. The cost you found out on a floor, with friends, if you were lucky, and in front of eight thousand people if you were not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:245:He sat with that for a while, and did not say it, because the other two knew it as well as he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:261:Lira turned her head on the boards and looked at Brom for a long moment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:297:"It is." Bracken lifted the glass again, looked, and lowered it. He was quiet for a while. Then he said, as if Cael had asked a question, which in a way he had: "The entry they wrote on you at Norhold had one word at its end. *Referred.* A referral is a letter, in my trade. A letter that has gone up a road gets an answer that comes down one." He turned the glass over in his hands. "I've kept this office for thirteen years. I've never yet seen an answer come down a road that I didn't see coming a minute before it reached the gate. I see no reason to begin now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:303:He did not say anything else, and Cael did not ask him to. They stood together at the high window for a while longer, a narrow grey man and a boy with a bad hip, looking at an empty road in the sun. A cart came up out of the trees on the lowest switchback, loaded with hay, a farmer walking at the horse's head. Bracken took the glass out again, and looked, and put it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:7:Nobody asked anything for a moment. The refectory went on round them, loud at the far end where the first-years were.
