editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:"Then they got the Fenmark sheet." She looked down at the bean and not at any of them. Everybody at that table knew what was at the bottom of her file, under four years of other people's honest lines, and nobody said it, and she did not say it either. After a moment she looked across at Cael. "Your column. Under my name. That they read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:83:Nobody touched it for a moment. Then Cael reached over and took it, and broke the fold, and read it where he sat, to himself, all of it, from the brass to the dog to the third paragraph and the single initial at the end. He read the third paragraph twice. Then he did not trust his voice, and he passed the sheet across the table to Brom, with his thumb under the third paragraph.
