editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:53:"Fifteen years of being sent to places. You don't do that without walking between them." Brom turned his spoon over. "I've done one road in my life that wasn't in a coach, and I did it badly, and I had Lira's blisters to carry as well as my own. I'd like to do the next one better. Whenever it is, and wherever." He did not look at any of them. "Can it be taught?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:133:He came down the track at the slow heavy walk he used for everything, in the very middle of it, with his big hands hanging open at his sides. He was not looking at the cottage; he was looking at the gardener, plainly, as he would have looked at a man across a counter. He stopped where the others had stopped, and stood there and waited, with the grey sky behind him, and did not shift his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:It did not ask, for the quiet thing from the stair never asked. It only offered, as it had offered every day of his life for two years, and it offered now with a kind of patience, as if it had been waiting since supper for him to see the sense of it. A man was sitting somewhere ahead in the dark, counting, and Cael was trying not to be counted; and here in him, sealed and paid for every morning since he woke, was the one thing he owned that could take him down that track past any man alive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:287:"Come tomorrow," she said. "Rooke'll let you, if I ask him. He let you stand in the door all week." She was not looking at him; she was looking across the empty court at the fountain. "Not the gallery. And not the rail either. The door, behind me, where I can't see you from my chalk. I want you there, and I don't want to look up and find you counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:219:"Two. The registrar told me about the letter to the council. I didn't ask him, he just told me, with his glasses off. If they give me the room, I'm keeping the lamp turned up in the window, every night, as high as it goes. I don't know what it was for and I'm not asking; I only know it was always lit, and the valley's used to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:95:He had counted the boy for a year. Two slips on a common-room fire. A season of figures that never once settled into a line. An afternoon at Norhold where everything held back went out in one breath, on purpose, in front of eight thousand people. He had counted all of it, and filed none of it, and somewhere in the counting, without asking his leave, it had turned into the other thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:The boy looked at the case and then at him, and did not reach for it, and did not ask what was in it. Seln had known that he would not. It was why he had climbed the stair. When the boy spoke at last it was quietly, to the case as much as to the man, and it was not quite a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:233:He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:69:"I left the counter," he said, without looking up. "Thirteen years and I have never left the counter. A first-year came to ask about a key and found nobody here." He dealt three to *OTHER*. "I have written it in the daybook. *Counter unattended, a quarter of an hour, by the registrar, on the registrar's own account.*" He dealt one to *FACULTIES*. "I was not going to leave that out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:79:He did not look up when Cael came in. He held out his left hand, without turning his head, with a small bundle of letters in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:"Somebody did it to me." She rested her chin on her crossed arms. "At a board, in front of her whole house. *Silver bracket, next cycle.* Then she turned round and walked off without looking back to see if I'd heard. People called it a challenge. It wasn't. A challenge is *I'll fight you*. That was *I'll be there*." She was quiet a while. "It's the most useful thing anybody's ever said to me. Four words. I don't think Zerin meant it kindly, and I don't think she cared whether it was kind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:169:The lodge was a single room built into the gatehouse wall, with a stove, a high stool, a counter, and a window on the road. The lodge-man was a short square man with a grey beard and a bad knee, who had kept the gate since before Bracken came to the hill. When Seln came in he was already pouring a second cup of tea, and he set it on the counter without being asked and without looking to see who it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:43:She did not look at Cael. She did not need to. The officer looked at him, once, the way a man checks a figure against the sum he was given, and looked away again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:101:"A registry instrument. Under escort. Served jointly on Withrow and me in the records hall ten minutes ago." He made himself say it in order, the way she would want it. "A challenge to the enrollment basis. Fourteen days to answer. Withrow read the first page aloud. Bracken is making you a copy now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:105:He told her the first page. He had it nearly whole; he had been listening the way he listened to anything that would have to be written down later. *Does not allege fraud. Nor contest the text.* *Evaluation integrity.* *Compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol.* *Stand or fall.* She listened to all of it without interrupting, with her pen still in her hand, and when he had finished she looked past him, down the narrow stair, for a long moment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:153:Behind them the records-hall door opened and Bracken came out onto the top step with a sheet of paper in his hand. It was green: a pale grey-green, like the underside of a willow leaf. He held it out at arm's length, away from himself, the way a man holds a thing he has found on his doorstep and is not yet sure is not alive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:117:"If he asked, I'd sit with him while he did. I'd read it to him if he wanted, and never say one word about it after." She climbed two more steps. "He won't ask. He's carrying it the way he carries everything, so nobody else has to. And the worst of it is that's exactly what we'd all want him to do, if it were any of us." She stopped at her own landing. "So we don't ask. And he carries it. And the letter sits in his coat getting heavier." She went in at her door and shut it quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:151:The girl waited, the way she had learned not to, for the rest. There was no rest. She went back to the outer table and wrote it in her own small notebook, and did not ask what it meant, and Cael did not hear anybody tell her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:161:Under Karis: *The fee office wants her standing before it will send her request on.* And under that, small, because the girl was not one of the four but there was nowhere else to put it: *Her assistant, on the walk. Answered with the code. Came back up the stair to say so.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:285:"Post," said Karis. She looked at the bag as another person might have looked at a loaf. "Every second day, I should think, this far out. I could have a reply from Ternhall in a fortnight from that hook."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:291:Cael ate at noon. It was not much, a heel of the guesting-house bread and a piece of cheese, but it stayed where he put it, and afterward he wanted more. Brom, who had been watching him with no attempt to hide it, took a second piece of cheese out of his own coat and held it out without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:301:"A fortnight. She said a fortnight." He turned the forearm in its strap. The plum had not faded, but the five bars across it had begun to blur at their edges, and he could close the hand halfway. "It's the only one I'll still be carrying past the last stone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:The records hall was dark except for the one lamp on the counter. Rain had not come yet, but it was in the air, and the tall windows along the east side showed nothing but grey. Bracken stood on the little step he kept under the counter for the purpose, with the clock's glass door open and the key in his hand, and turned it eleven times. He always turned it eleven times. Seln had counted it on perhaps eighty Seventh-days and had never once heard a twelfth. Then the registrar shut the glass, stepped down, put the step away with his foot, and looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:87:He stood a moment in the shop doorway with the twine in his hand, out of the rain, and watched the street. A dray going over the bridge with its load sheeted. Two boys with a dog. A woman under a shawl buying eels from a barrow. The bridge-ward in his oilskin at the near end of the parapet, looking down at the water against the old piers and then at the iron rings let into the stone of the middle one, the way a man looks at a clock he expects to be late. There were five rings on that pier, one above the other. The river was at the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The seat wanted the maker of the copies to swear to them, and the advocate of record to sign beside his oath. Karis had said at supper that she would not put her name to a page she had not read against the thing it was copied from, and that this was not caution but manners. So the wing's original cards had come up the library stair in two shallow boxes under the porter's oilcloth, with Seln's leave and Bracken's receipt, and the second set of Seln's copies lay open beside them. He had made two of everything. One had gone down to the seat in the house's boards. The other had stayed in the annex, against exactly this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:151:Lira was on his bed with her boots off and her back against the wall and her feet crossed at the ankle. She had come in without knocking, as she always did, and sat down without asking, as she always did. Brom had the chair, which was too small for him, and it had complained when he sat in it, and he had sat in it anyway with great care, the way he did everything to furniture. Karis stood. She had a sheaf of the attestation papers under her arm and had not let go of them since supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:171:"If it's her," he said. "If it's anybody like her. If somebody begins a fight with me, in earnest, the way she would begin one. I want it priced before, not after. I want the whole leaf written tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:183:The covenant was at the front of Hesk's book. He did not need to turn to it to know what it said, but he turned to it anyway and laid the book open on the sill where they could all see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:245:She did not ask. She leaned off the bed and took it, and pulled the book toward her across the sill, and wrote one more line under his in her square capitals, pressing hard, the way she always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:25:Burst one, low and flat off the left foot, not across the glide but along the inside of its curve, the line she had found on this floor before the first sitting and beaten this girl's pattern with on more mornings than either of them could count. It was right, and Cael saw that it was right from the door. At the far end of that line there was a place on the boards where the Current girl's bend would bring her hip, and Lira would be standing in that place a quarter of a beat before the hip arrived, with her palm out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:31:The landing beat was late. It was not late by much, only by the length of a step, but the whole of Lira's fighting was built on that beat, the instant after a burst when the floor took her weight and gave it back and she could go again. She had it on the left foot, half a pace past where she had meant to be, facing a little the wrong way. The place where the hip was going to be was behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:53:At the far rail the Force unfolded his arms and folded them again the other way. Rooke wrote something on his board. Cael let out a breath he had not known he was holding, and Lira, walking back, opened and shut her taped hands twice, quickly, the way she did when she was pleased with herself and did not mean to show it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:59:Cael had fought her, and knew she would. She had come to wing three with two long glides and a bend, and when he had taken those away she had found a straight glide, and after that a glide broken by a short hard step, all inside one bout. She was not a clever fighter, but she was a quick one, and she learned on her feet. Now she came off her chalk in short pieces, a glide and a step and a glide, never more than six feet of anything, so that there was no long curve to get inside and no moment when her weight was laid out for a lie to pull.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:61:Lira read it. He saw her read it, on the girl's front hip, in the first two pieces. She did not burst, but kept the last one in her pocket and walked the exchange instead, on her feet, cutting corners, trying to be where the next short glide would end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:69:It was the Current girl who found it. Cael saw her find it. She stood at the north chalk before the fourth with her hands loose and her head a little on one side, looking not at Lira's face or her shoulders but at her feet, as a person looks at a sum on a slate that has come out the same three times. Then her eyes went, once, to a place on the boards to the left of where Lira was standing.

