editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:113:He had a piece of her. He went on knowing it the whole time he copied, the way you go on knowing there is a stone in your boot. Somewhere under his ribs, settled since the first morning of the year, sat the weight he had taken from her on the floor at Norhold: the lane, the leaning air, the read that at her weight could have the air or his body and not both. He had paid for it on a stone floor with his whole left side, and he paid for it still, ten counts slow every time he laid a lane. She did not know she had given it. She never would. And here she was in four lines, telling him how to keep what she had never known she was giving away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:3:Karis ate nothing on the morning of the second closing, and Brom carried her breakfast down the hill in his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:7:In the coach she sat with the wallet on her knees and did not open it. She had the twenty-five slips by heart; he had heard her say them through twice more since the east hall, once to the reading room's window and once, at midnight, to the landing, when she thought the stair was empty. She looked out at the switchbacks going by, and her fingers lay on the leather, quite still, and paler than the rest of her hand, as they would be for the rest of her life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:15:The market street was full. People stood on the four steps of the wool exchange and down the plinth and out across the cobbles as far as the trough, so that the coachman had to walk his horses through them to the stable arch with the brake half on. Faces turned to the glass as they went by, and nobody shouted anything. It was a wool town, and it knew what a closing was; you came, and stood, and heard which way the price had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:229:"Twenty-six years," said the counsel. She was looking up, with her small grey eyes very bright, and she still had hold of the sleeve. "Twenty-six years in front of seats, and I have heard perhaps four closings I should have been glad to sign. That is the best argument I have ever heard made in one of those rooms, and I include every one of mine. I want you to have that from somebody who is paid to know." She let go. "It will not have moved him. You knew that. You did it as though you didn't, and that is the whole of the trade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:37:He came out of the infirmary with the linen triangle folded small in his good hand and his left arm hanging at his side, and stood in the covered walk opening and closing the left hand very slowly, the way a man tries a hinge that has been painted shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:311:Cael stood in the middle of the painted floor and looked at Brom's hand on the edge of the door, the big square hand that had held twelve feet of road against two men on the Ostrand road and held a girl up in a doorway for twelve seconds. Something in him went quiet that had nothing to do with the new thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:341:She put the two flames out with a closing of her hand, and stood, and moved the line. She drew it again two feet further from him, and lit its ends again, and counted three and four, and the new thing asked, and he named it. She moved it two feet further and counted five and six, and it asked a little less surely, as a voice is less sure across a wider room. At ten feet he named it, and Karis wrote *10* in her notebook and drew a ring round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:343:At twelve feet she lit the seventh and the eighth, and the asking was very faint, like somebody speaking in the next room with the door shut. At fourteen she drew the line and did not light it, and asked him, and there was nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:351:She went back to the line at twelve feet, to put out the last two flames, and it happened then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:353:The chalk line at twelve feet lay on the boards between its two small fires, and the new thing, which had been faint about it, was suddenly not faint. It leaned. It leaned the way it had leaned in his own doorway with Lira coming through it on her crutch, when he had been halfway through saying *no*. Something in him began to go out of him toward the line, quick and quiet as a breath, to set a point at one end of it and a point at the other and tell the space between them to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:43:"And reach is what they can't have," Karis went on, "because reach is what doesn't fit a column. A child who reads Current and something of Mire isn't a better Current. She's a question the registry hasn't got a box for. So the function closes the question before anybody asks it. Margin reading not to be entered. And she goes home with one family on her paper and grows into the paper, because people do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:347:"Dry," she said. She said it twice more. Then she sat down on the wet bank with her back against the wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:It was the only use he could find for the quarter hour before the seat came in. The coach had gone down the bluff road at the usual pace behind Withrow's carriage, and the two coats at the road's foot had looked at it as they looked at it every Fifth-day, and the young officer at the side door had written the same two lines in his log. But the exchange's four steps had been crowded to the plinth this morning, and the benches behind Cael were filling with a noise he had not heard in that room since the preliminary. A closing, the house's counsel had said, was the one day besides the first when a proceeding sold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:23:The clerks in their grey had come to see their own seat do its work. The correspondents would write down what was said, and get some of it wrong, and mean none of the errors. The four evaluators were working officers who had read every exhibit they were handed. Ilsev had asked the seat to minute the edge of her own chair, so that nobody could push her past it. At the records table sat a man who had receipted a hundred and seventy-nine leaves without losing one. And Jent, with his travelling inkwell and his mended cuffs, had given away every point that was not true as soon as he was shown it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:141:"Not for the floor," she said. She had come to Lira's door with the wallet under her arm and Hesk's clock in her hand, which she had taken off Cael's shelf without asking. "For the room. I've been saying it to a pane of glass, and a pane of glass doesn't sit there in a hood deciding not to like me. I want to say it standing up, to people, with somebody timing who isn't me." She held out the clock. "It loses. Add two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:165:Seln had not come onto the floor. He stood at the great door with one hand on its edge, holding it, as Brom had held it on the night they measured ten feet; he had said on the stair that he would take the door tonight, since the seat could not. He watched the blanket for a moment with no expression at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:211:He stood by the great door a moment after the others had gone through it, with the clock ticking in his hand and losing its minutes. Seln still held the door, and the lanterns were out behind them. He had laughed tonight, at a haystack in a chair, until his ribs remembered the road. He could not think when he had last laughed like that on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:15:"All of us," he said. "Before the year closes. By a door that's in the code, and in an old enrollment book. Karis found it in the term before the recess, and it leaves nothing in that folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:83:"There is no form for taking a leaf out of that folder," she said. "I told you so. There is also no rule against putting one in. I have a closing in the registry's presses under an enrollee's name, which I can cite, and a magister's note for the seventy-sixth day. I have thirty-four chancellors who wrote to ask me what I would do, and who now owe me the courtesy of reading what I did. And I have a right-hand column in a great many places besides this one." She came round the end of the desk. "I do not know what took you up that stair tonight with four others. I am not a fool, and I can see that it was more than a ruling, and I am not going to ask. Bracken asks when. I find I do not want even that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:87:"No. I said in the term that I'd be annoyed if you thanked me, and I should be." She had her hand on the door. "The captain came to me at supper to ask for the Crown yard tomorrow at the fourth bell. I told him the yard was Rooke's to give and the bout was yours, and I see by your face that he has since asked you. I shall be in the stand. I have sat under a rug in other people's halls for two years to watch my own fighters, and I should like to see one thing on my own oak before the year closes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:99:He sat at the sill with Hesk's clock losing its minutes on the shelf behind him and turned to a clean leaf. He had priced the eighth on a leaf like this, in the week of the rain, and four people had put their initials to it, and he had not thought then that he would ever price another. He wrote it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:315:Cael saw the lath waiting and took a fifth burst out of the air above it, sideways, off nothing but the landing beat of the fourth, and came down ten feet clear with his hip beginning to write. Five on the oak, and the day's free six were nearly gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:337:Ephram did not know about the ten counts; nobody on the bluff did but three people on a tier. But he was the best Iron on the hill and he could see a man who had gone a beat late, and he came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:13:The stranger was a Stone, thick through the shoulders and slow in the feet, and he stood at the near end of the painted lines with his hands folded in front of him like a man waiting outside a shop. Cael had seen him last at the wool town, on the far side of a rope, losing to Lira. He had been an Iron of the river academy then, its first entry, and she had beaten him by finding the half a beat in which neither of his planted feet was reading the floor, and going through it. Now he was a Silver, newly advanced, and he had sewn nothing on his collar to say so. Over the recess, Lira had said, he had written to Rooke and asked for the posting, and brought his academy's drill-book with him in a red board cover so that the bluff could copy it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:139:Behind the counter, the desk clerk sat at his ledger by the window, adding a column under his breath and losing it whenever the Current girls' whispering rose. Gault's door stood shut, with voices behind it. And behind the counter at the copying table, rising every time the queue moved to take the next sheet, the teaching assistant did the counter's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:Lira lost the first two exchanges. She lost them cleanly and quickly and said "Again" both times before she had finished losing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:95:"I'll need to mark my place. Two hours is long for a gallery." The counsel laid the rule flat on the table, pointing at the window, and turned it a quarter of the way round, and then another quarter, as if she were trying the weight of the idea. "That will do. A turn for each. Anyone who loses the thread can look at the rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:It was the names that had made the evaluators look up. Ilsev heard her own read out among them and wrote something small in her file without lifting her head. The counsel read twenty-two of them, one at a time, each with a grade and a house and a day, at the pace at which a person reads names in a room where names matter. The grant at Greyvane and its two renewals on paper, the season's officers under a regional registrar's seal, the tournament's, and three days of the Compact's own senior evaluator on the bluff. At the Archmarshal's name Cael thought the stillness under the hood altered, the way still water alters when something passes under it. It took four minutes. In the quiet he heard a cart's iron tyre grind over the cobbles in the street. More looking, the counsel said when she had finished, than most practitioners get in a working life; and all of it done by people who could see, sealed and minuted, across three years. She turned the rule. Tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:209:*Argument on the petition to compel the enrollee's re-evaluation under the standard Arbiter protocol is set down for the sittings that remain, and the seat will rule on it before the academic year closes. The matter proceeds in full: how far the integrity sections reach a practitioner is for the registry to determine, and the seat determines that they reach this one far enough to be heard. On the question raised, the seat may take it up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:27:On the shelf over the table stood the first manual and the second, and beside them a space. The dust lay a little differently there, in a clean oblong about the length of a forearm, and the clerk looked at it and it told him nothing. He could not have said what had stood in that place. He had sat ten feet from it for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:217:The light went slowly. He had water in the can and the can on its stone, and he had walked forty of somebody else's paces twice more, to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:"Because every one of them costs," he said. "The Wind's bill comes to the hip. The push comes out of the right shoulder, four blows a sitting and no more. The read is cheap, and the give is a cup that fills. Karis's spark burns me too, at the fingers, if I spend it past two contacts. The storm's the heaviest thing I own, and when I lay a lane the whole of me goes a beat slow afterward for ten counts, and if I lay three close together the third costs twelve." He said it in the log's voice, flat and exact. "Those are the prices, and they're all in the book. Nothing's free. That was the first thing I learned, on a cot at Ardenmere with the Log on my knee, and it's still true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:99:"You may say it to me twice more in your life," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:224:They went down at a little before the tenth bell, one at a time, a few minutes apart, because Seln said that five people coming down a stair together was a meeting, and five people coming down one at a time was the library closing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:That was all. He did not seem to think it needed more, and after a moment Cael understood that it did not. Brom had held twelve feet of road with one arm, and had sat a rota outside a door for two nights without being asked. Of course it was Brom who counted, and counted again when the first count was right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:He wrote it at the sill, most nights, by the lamp, with Hesk's clock losing its minutes on the shelf behind him and the court dark below. He wrote less than he had ever written in a term, for there was a great deal he could not put in a book, and what was left over was so much alike from one Fifth-day to the next that he caught himself, more than once, turning back a leaf to make sure he had not already entered it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:43:"Then your closing goes in as a written submission, every word, under your name, and I read out as much of it as the hood will sit through." She said it quite gently. "It will be in the minute either way. I can't promise you the room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:91:"The merits are concluded. The seat sets the matter down for closing." The unhurried voice went on, with no more weight on these words than on any others it had spoken that year. "Two closing sittings, in the last month of the academic year, a week apart. The ruling will be given upon the last Fifth-day of that month. The parties will be notified of the hours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:171:*Closing sitting: the second Fifth-day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:173:*Closing sitting: the third Fifth-day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:251:*The seat: two closing sittings and a ruling, the last on the month's last Fifth-day. Leave for Karis to be heard is "to be considered at the close." Brom's panel sits the same day, at the second bell, three streets off.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:3:The house kept no rest day in its last week, and the closing seminars sat through the Seventh-day as they did every year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:265:She had come down from her own range on her own feet. He had known her come to that gate once before, for four riders, when she had stood where she stood now, in the twelve feet of shadow under the gatehouse, and had an instrument read aloud. She had nothing in her hands today.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:135:The girl shut the door. "At the end she said *thank you* in a very small voice and went away. Before that she kept looking at the stair." She swallowed. "Before that, I'd told her about the ninety-one. And the paragraph numbers. And that the next sitting's on the Fifth-day, and that the petitioner's conceded the provision is valid and doesn't allege fraud, and that the Compact's own inspection looked at the provision and it stood." She was going backward through it as Karis had told her to, and her voice steadied as she went. "And at the start I told her he's enrolled under a lawful provision, adjudicated at Greyvane on the public record, under seal, with the grant and two renewals on paper. Because she asked me what the enrollee was *really* like. A Mire girl from the north stair, on the covered walk, after supper. I hardly know her." She stopped. "That was the beginning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:45:"There's a word you'll hear about this all year," he said. "In corridors. Behind doors. Said by people who think it's a kindness to keep it from you. I'd sooner you had it from me, across a table, now. Seven of these dockets have crossed my counter in thirteen years, and seven times the panel found for the tier the title was won at. Your name is cut on a staff in the middle of the continent where any carter can read it, and no district office alive would care to be caught reading you lower than that staff. It is a formality. You'll go into that room Copper and come out of it Iron. Your family's ledger closes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:71:It was the best case there was, and Cael, with the pen in his hand, found that some part of him had leaned toward it while she spoke. It was the old pole, the one he had written in his best hand on the first night: *The system has text, and text has held me.* The most honest reader he knew had just told him, in her closing voice, that there was one more text.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:93:"It would read as manufactured," said Lira. "You said that yourself, Karis, in a coach, about a road with six people on it and bruises to show. This is paper. An unclassified enrollee loses at a seat on the Fifth-day, and on the Sixth he turns up with a box of old orders that say the whole registry's crooked." She shook her head. "I wouldn't believe us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:181:"Before the year closes," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:281:"We're going," said Cael. "All five of us. Before the year closes, by a door that leaves the house clean. East."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:193:"I know you're all right. It's still the last one." She put the pencil away. "You'd only ever met the ten counts by themselves. Tonight you met them three times in a row and the third ran long. That's a new figure. We don't spend a new figure the night we find it. Rooke says that." She almost smiled. "Rooke says it about me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:211:It was the first new thing the lane had told him since the morning it settled. He had believed the ten counts were the price, fixed, like the five free bursts on boards; a thing he could plan around, write in a minute before a fight, and pay. Now he knew they were not fixed. They grew when he spent the lane over and over. He did not know yet by how much, or whether they grew without end, or whether they came back down with rest. He knew only that a figure he had thought was a wall was a slope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:55:It was in the boy's file in the same drawer, the clerk's ruled page from the room twelve feet by fourteen on the bluff. The first thing his eye went to, as it always did, was not any of his questions but the line near the foot where one was written going the other way, the boy's to him: *What is the Compact evaluating? Me, or the provision?* The second thing was the line beneath his own eleventh question. He had ruled it himself, before the interview, for a twelfth, the way he always ruled one more line than he expected to use. It was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:209:"Rooke told me last night, while you were all asleep," she said, between spoonfuls, "that losing the first on purpose has a name in the program. He said it's called honesty." She pointed the spoon at Cael. "Write that down. He calls it honesty. I'm going to call it the ropes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:91:Cael watched him. He had seen that face across a counter for two years and had never once seen it lose, and it was losing now, to three people at a fire; and he thought that somewhere under the mildness the man was more pleased than he had been since a birthday.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:He had read her four times. The first two he already had: three paces in, onto the one level patch in the court. The third time he had asked the court what she could see from there, and the answer had been the arch. The gatehouse was twelve feet deep. From almost anywhere in the court you saw only a slice of the road through it, the left side from the covered walk, the right from the lecture range. There were a few feet of flag from which the whole opening lay open end to end, road and verges and the first bend below the gate. She had stood in them with her back three-quarters to the arch, and never once turned to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:157:Cael had drawn the outer court at its own size, fifty feet by seventy, paced once by him and once by Brom until they agreed. The arch as two long lines, twelve feet apart. The lodge door in its cheek. The covered walk's corner. The mouth of the lane. The stable passage, with no door drawn across it. In the middle, a square for the capped well and a ring round it for the level flags; and Brom, before the chalk was out of Cael's hand, had walked into the ring three paces from the arch and turned his shoulders a hand's breadth toward the stable passage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:181:Brom considered the chalk arch, twelve feet deep, and the open stable passage. After a while he said, "I'd stand there too, if I was holding it. I'd never have found it in three steps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:119:"Forty-four," she said. "It loses, doesn't it? Call it forty-six."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:"Good." He nodded at the pine boards, the trestle, Gault closing his case. "The Mire's a decent fighter. He'll think he gave you trouble." He started for the door, and stopped with his hand on it, and did not turn round. "He didn't, did he."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:259:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. The evaluation seat constituted for these proceedings, having first given notice in the year's eighth week that certification would be required, now renews its request to the Archmarshal's office for certification of the standard protocol, as applied to a re-evaluation of the enrollee of record at Halcenvane, should such be ordered. The seat's closing sittings are set down for the year's last month. The office's certificate is requested before the first of them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:171:They were not with him. They never came to a sitting. They were in his kit at the inn, between the second manual and the third, where they had lived since the night in Norhold when he had written the fifth on a narrow table behind the east tower, with the line he had read upside down on the senior seat's desk at its head. Five entries. The first was a pump step at Ardenmere, and a form with four questions on it, and a mark in the administrative layer of the boy's file that had no office behind it. The last was a sentence closing a door that the first had found open. In between were three more, and every one of the five recorded something that had happened, and nothing that he made of it. That was the rule of the sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:237:*The month closes, and for once the people I came up this hill with have each been handed something fair. Lira has her ladder, a point short, with the point named. Brom has his number, written on a docket a street from the seat. Karis has a shelf, and a key, and a girl to teach, and a line in a book with her name above it. None of it bought. None of it begged. All of it given across a counter, honestly, for once.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:249:Seln's eyes stayed shut. It was Karis who answered, with her finger on his line. "From the day the fourth sitting closes. Before the fortieth day, a respondent who hasn't come back is only absent. On the fortieth, the code changes the entry to *fled*." She looked at the figure as if she had never seen one before. "It's a narrow thing. I had it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:He priced it at his sill, by the lamp, in four lines. *Night, the east hall, L. alone. Everything public, at the rate, to win. Buys: she knows. Spends: hip past five on boards; shoulder; ten counts for a lane, twelve for a third; behind the eyes. Not the quiet thing. Not the new one.* He looked at *she knows* for a moment and left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:81:Then the wall was at his back, with a flight of steps on his right hand, and a line lay on each side of him. Ephram stood ten feet off, and his heel was up. There was one place left, and both of them knew where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:266:*He said "non-hostile" and the system agreed. Nine fragments and the ninth one's engagement field says the closest thing to friendship a machine knows how to file. I'll take it. It's the truest thing the instrument ever wrote about me except "no number goes here," and it was written about a fight I didn't want to win.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:163:They were standing along the walls, and the doors had been left open so that the steps could hear, and the steps were full, and past them the street. Everybody who had come down for the closing had come again, and Ephram's cohort had the ninth and tenth benches, and this time nobody had needed to threaten them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:223:"I want one thing said before the hill gets hold of it," she said. "I read losing filings for twenty years at the registry. It was my work to say why they had lost. They lost because a date was wrong, or a section was misread, or somebody had hoped a seat would not notice a thing it was paid to notice." She looked at Karis, and then, longer, at Cael. "You did not lose badly. I should like that to be the first thing either of you remembers about today, and I shall say it to anybody who asks me. There is not a line in that ruling that answers a line of yours. It could not answer them. It went round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:235:He rubbed out the three lines with the heel of his hand, the two closings and the ruling, and left the fourth, because that was Brom's and not the house's. He wrote in their place, square and plain:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:7:He had Hesk's book open on the sill and Hesk's steel pen in his hand. Hesk had raised him in an instrument-maker's workshop at the far end of the country, and had bound the book for him with his own hands, and it held the Log now, the record where Cael set down each fact with its price beside it. Hesk's travelling clock sat at his elbow, losing time in its usual direction, a little more of the hour every day. In the tournament weeks he had set it an hour fast on purpose so that it would be right by accident, and on finals morning he had put it back himself. Since the road it had gone on losing at its own pace. He told the hour by the fountain and the bell, and kept the clock for the minutes, which it counted honestly whatever it thought of the hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:It was not in the hip and it was not in the shoulder. It was behind the eyes, a heaviness, as if somebody had laid a warm hand over the inside of his skull. And everything else he carried went slow with it. He reached for the Wind, out of curiosity, the way you test a stair, and it came, but it came late: a beat late, the way a dog comes when it has decided to come in its own time. He counted. For ten counts after the lane, everything answered him a beat behind his asking. At eleven it was gone, and the hand had lifted off the inside of his head, and the Wind came when he called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:199:He told her exactly: behind the eyes, ten counts of everything slow, gone at eleven. She wrote it on her hand under the shoulder and the hip, small, because she was running out of hand. Then she stopped writing and looked up at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:209:She wrote *bill, not flag* under the ten counts, and underlined it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:219:He had it by heart. Every notice he had ever had he knew by heart, because each of them had found him somewhere with nothing else to look at, a yard, a stair, a shut room, and the one from Norhold on one knee on a stone floor, with a healer holding his hand. *Note: pressure-differential component; corridor-seeding component. Short range.* At the time it had read like a clerk's description of somebody else's coat. Tonight he thought he could put his hand on each line of it. The lane was the corridor; he seeded it, and it grew where he put it, and stayed while he left it alone. The tilt of the air, the ears, the dust running downhill, that was the pressure. Short range was fifteen feet. And the notice had said nothing at all about the read going into the air and leaving the body behind, or about ten counts of everything slow. Notices never told you what a thing cost. They only told you what it was. The cost you found out on a floor, with friends, if you were lucky, and in front of eight thousand people if you were not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:269:Lira held up her hand to the lantern and read it aloud, squinting at the pencil marks running across her palm and down onto her wrist. "Hip, crest to knee, the eighth into the thigh. Shoulder, five, the fifth counted. Forearm: four in a row, the fourth to the elbow; it's a cup, not a wall. Spark: one, on Brom, who said hm. The lane: fifteen feet, a third deep. First time, the read went into the air and Brom put him on the floor. Second time, lane first, read second, through. After: behind the eyes, ten counts slow, gone at eleven." She lowered the hand. "One drift. Held." She looked at her palm. "Karis is going to make me copy this out three times because of my capitals."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:He was a young man in a wet hat, and he apologised three times on the stair before he got to the door, and twice more inside it. The district seat's records room was a man short, he said. Two men, if you counted the one with the fever. A letter had come in from the registrar of the academy on the bluff, about an order and a seal, and it concerned the proceedings, and the senior records officer had said that the proceedings' own records support was in the town on its days off with nothing to do, and would the junior chair be so good as to check the registers and draft the usual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:71:He read them through. The first went back to a pump step at Ardenmere and a form of four questions, and to the mark in a boy's file that no office would own. The fifth was a sentence on a senior seat's desk at Norhold, read upside down, closing a door that the first had found open. In between were three more. He read them all twice, slowly, the way the manual said a records officer should read anything he meant to rely on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:205:Together they made a frame. He saw its shape as he knelt there, as clearly as he had seen the chalk court on the oak. Both mouths of the cut were closed. The west mouth behind them had three points across it, and the east had the cart and the culvert and the milestone. The north bank was too high. The middle of the road was held. There was one way through the whole cut that the points did not close, and it was a channel along the foot of the south bank, under the overhanging lip, perhaps twelve feet wide, running east past the cart's tail to the culvert.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:He looked at the hobbles and the folded hood and the soft rope, and the staves that had been chosen because they bruised and did not cut, and the six faces that were not faces. He looked at the channel along the south bank, twelve feet wide, running to the culvert. He thought of the gully beyond it, which he could not see into, and what a man would keep there if he had come to take a person somewhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:135:"The second paragraph," she said, before anybody asked. "*Of the seat's hearing, and who may be heard.* The advocate of record heard at the close, by the seat's leave. The house's counsel thinks the seat will give it, if it's asked properly and early." She squared the first row with the side of her hand. "So I'm writing a closing. In bones, for now. The flesh after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:143:"I know what a closing's for." Lira put the book down. "I mean what for, now. We know what reads him. We know the seat runs on it, and so does the evaluation, and the re-evaluation's the delivery, whichever way it goes. Your bones are going to stand up in front of a hood that already knows what it's going to say." She said it carefully, because she was fond of Karis and Karis was not looking at her. "I'm not saying don't. I'm asking what it's for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:179:"Because of the coastal man," said Cael. "The one who wrote his letter and went home. The default quoted him. His own words, in the second paragraph, as the reason." He looked at Karis, because she had read it to them in the dairy. "If I stop going, they don't have to prove anything. I prove it for them. I walk out of the room and they write down that I walked, and that's their whole case, signed by me." He shook his head. "I'm not going to hand them that. If they want me in a frame, they'll have to put me in it with their own hands, in their own minute, with Karis's closing standing in front of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:"Then what am I writing?" she said. Her voice was very level. "If it isn't ground. If the closing is a speech to a floor somebody laid where they liked." She looked at her slips, and Cael saw that her pale fingers were pressed flat on the cloth. "I've built everything I am on paper being worth reading properly. You've just told me it isn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:199:"I said I don't stand on it anymore." He held her eyes. "I didn't say it's not worth writing. They'll read your closing, and the minute will have it, and when they rule they'll rule against it in writing. That's not nothing. It's just not ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:"Until the year closes. A pattern that changes is a thing somebody writes down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:187:*Assay-provision enrollment: resigned in good standing prior to revocation date. Baseline file: closed complete. Four terms of evaluation-linked observation, no adverse finding, no anomaly of conduct. The evaluator of record notes that the file closes on the enrollee's own act and not the registry's, and that this is the correct closing, and the only just one available. The instruments will not see its like.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:There was a bed, and a press, and a table, and a sill, and he had lived among them for two years and the better part of a third. Seln had given the rule in an orchard: forty pounds for a day, and thirty for thirty days. Cael's came to less than thirty. He folded two shirts and the spare coat, and rolled the strapping the healer had left him, and put Hesk's clock into a stocking; it was a quarter of an hour behind the bell, and losing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:135:His right hand was opening and closing a little, under the shelf, as if it ached. He did not look up at either of them, and after a moment he drew the next thing in his tray toward him.

