editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:17:The instructor was a spare grey woman with chalk on both cuffs, who had sat on district panels for twenty years before she taught, and who spoke as she must have scored, without ornament.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:19:"Control," she said. "We finish where we began. You have all been told that a panel marks control on the landing, and that is not so. A panel marks what it can see of the landing from where it has been put, and it has generally been put on the east side, out of the sun. So you will be marked on the half of you that faces a table. Write that down, and then write down what follows from it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:She wrote quickly and without looking up. The instructor went on, about a fighter who chose her foot late and a table that would read the choosing as a fault, and how many exchanges it took a good panel to learn otherwise, and Lira turned a leaf. She asked one question, which he could not hear, and was answered at some length, and wrote the answer down as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:27:It came to him, on the bench, what she was doing. She was not sitting out a class for the look of it. She would never stand before a district's panel again, and she knew it better than anybody; but there was a road to the east with nobody's table on it, and somebody would have to be the east side for her out there, and she was taking down how it was done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:49:The notes were real, and that had surprised her a little. She had meant to sit a quarter of an hour, for the form of it, and say her sentence. Then the grey woman had begun on what a table could see and what it couldn't, and Lira had found the pencil in her hand, because it was all true and nobody had ever said it to her so plainly. *A panel sees half of you.* She had turned to the back of the carter's book, under *ME*, and taken it down as fast as she could write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:91:"It had better be." She tucked the book inside her coat. "I'm going down for the horse. Don't let Brom start without a cart to put them in."
