editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:299:"I'll remember it," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:311:The gelding liked the going down no better than the coming up. Lira leaned on his cheek-strap and told him that it was the last hill of the day, while Brom had the tailboard, with one hand on the canvas, and Karis walked behind the cart with her eyes on the load and did not once look back at the roofs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:331:The ferry was waiting, with its lantern lit. The ferryman looked at the cart and named a price, and Brom paid it, and they crossed the Ost on the brown water with the gelding's eyes rolling. On the far bank Ostrand had its windows alight, street above street, and somewhere among them was a wool exchange with a lot number on its floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:335:He wrote the day's line there, sitting on the press's platen with the book on his knee, where Brom had stood under that beam some days ago and held what it let down on him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:339:*Seventh-day. Four leaves at Bracken's counter, in the order we came: Lira, Brom, me, Karis. Entered and blotted. Nothing left on the hill with our names on it but what's true.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:5:So the three of them stood inside the records-hall door and waited. Cael counted the turns from habit, and Brom counted them beside him, and at the eleventh Bracken took the key out of the clock's face and hung it on its nail. He set the hands by his own watch and shut the glass. Then he turned round to his counter and looked at what was on it, as though somebody else might have laid it there in the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:39:Brom came up second, and stood a moment with his hands at his sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:43:"That's the one," said Brom, when he had looked at it for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:51:Brom nodded slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:61:Brom's face did not change. But he put two fingers on the counter's edge for a moment, as Rooke put two fingers to a board, and then he stepped back and made room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:71:*Enrollee: Caelen Hesk-ward.* It was the name the registry had given him at Denvash, the one that stood on every instrument and that nobody who knew him had ever used, and Bracken had written it out in full, because a form was an instrument. *House: Halcenvane. Basis of enrollment: the demonstration provision of the assay clause; granted at Greyvane; twice renewed.* And on the line for tier, where Lira's leaf said *Iron* and Brom's said *Copper*, the registrar had written the only true thing there was to write, as he had seen it written on a door log at a wool exchange every Fifth-day for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:89:He wrote in the margin. It was not the upright hand of his entries but the other, the sloped longhand of his private daybook, which Cael had seen perhaps four times, and he wrote three lines of it, slowly and close, running up the edge of the form where the registry had printed nothing. Then he turned the leaf back, and Cael read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:95:"There is a margin. A margin belongs to the officer who takes the paper, and I have been thirteen years learning what one is for." Bracken laid the pen down. "Somebody will open this, some year. He will see a challenge, and a ruling, and a withdrawal inside the same week, and he will ask himself the natural question, which is whether the house put you out to save itself. He will be a careful man, I hope, and I should like him to find that the registrar was there, and looked at the clock, and wrote down what he saw." He paused. "The chancellor knew of this door four days before you did, and she did not say one word to you about it. I know what that cost, for I was the one she did not send. It ought to be written somewhere that nobody pushed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:"It is," said Bracken. He had a second paper ready beside her leaf, and it ran to a full sheet. "An enrollee who holds standing under the research council does not only withdraw. She lapses, by heads, and the house is bound to read her the heads, so that nobody may say afterward that a thing was taken from her unawares. I drew it from the council's minute, and I believe it agrees with a sheet of your own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:117:"I made mine in the order the council granted them. I thought you would rather hear it go backward than have it put in an order you could correct."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:129:"She was never mine. There's a letter to the council, and you have it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:"I have it. It is on the council's table, on top." He made a mark. "*The house's archive privileges, and under the house's seal those of the archives at Ternhall and at Greyvane.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:Behind the counter the pigeonholes ran in their rows, each with a card. The seventh had said the same thing since the week the council voted: *Research standing: floor and archive hours, by application.* A green form had come out of it once, for a night with the lamps lit in pairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:"There is one line more on the council's minute," he said, and he took up the sheet again. "*The enrollee's tier, as certified by the registry: Ember; Iron, Rank Three.* I have not read it to you, and I have struck it from this schedule, in ink, with my initials against the stroke, because it is not the house's. It was never the house's to grant, and it does not lapse with a room." He looked at her over the glasses. "Nobody can take a tier from you, Advocate. They can only decline to count it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:167:Bracken gathered the four leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:169:He squared them against the counter's edge, and laid Karis's schedule under them, and set the house's seal on the pile to hold it. Then he stood with both hands on the wood and did not reach for the daybook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:"Tomorrow, at the first bell, I shall open the daybook and enter the post. On the next Seventh-day I shall wind that clock. In the week after the close a clerk will come up the road with a stamp for the chancellor's folder, and I shall see that he is handed the right volume and initials his own line." He took the last pair of glasses off his forehead and laid all three in a row. "I tell you so because you might suppose, after this year, that a man in my chair would go about it differently. I have asked myself whether I ought. I intend to keep true records, as I have kept them, and four files will go down to the district in tomorrow's bag with not a line in them that I could not swear to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:185:He drew a single small leaf from under the daybook and turned it round. It was Gault's hand, the panel-note hand, as square as a ruled table, which Cael had seen once a week for two years at the foot of three cards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:219:"Two. The registrar told me about the letter to the council. I didn't ask him, he just told me, with his glasses off. If they give me the room, I'm keeping the lamp turned up in the window, every night, as high as it goes. I don't know what it was for and I'm not asking; I only know it was always lit, and the valley's used to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:223:"Three," said the assistant, and now her voice shook. "You never wrote to me. There's a letter for the council and none for me, and I know, because I looked in every place you'd have left one." She unfolded her hands and folded them the other way. "I'm glad. You'd have got it exactly right, and I'd have had to keep it for the rest of my life and never be cross with you. This way I can be cross. That's the third thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Ephram did not walk this time. He came across the oak like a man who has been shown at last what he is fighting, and means to have as much of it as there is, with the sun behind him now and the lath a dark bar against it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:11:*Only inside a fight the other begins, in earnest, awake, and on purpose.* Ephram had begun it in a reading room at midnight, with five people listening, and he had grounded his lath in front of twelve hundred rather than be given less than all of it. Nobody had ever been more in earnest who did not also wish him harm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:13:*The whole cost on paper first.* It was on a leaf in Hesk's book, in the satchel on the east tier at Brom's feet, with capitals across the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:23:"You're letting me," said Ephram, between his teeth. He had checked, and stood a pace off with the lath raised and the heel not yet down, breathing hard, and he looked angrier than he had looked all afternoon. "I said I wanted all of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:27:Ephram stared at him. Then something went out of his shoulders, and what came into his face instead was not softer, but it was pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:He cut in earnest. Cael saw the whole engine of it, close, in the moment before it reached him: the heel that committed the body, and the hip that carried it, and the edge that could not then be called back by the man who had sent it. And he saw the other thing, which he had never understood from the rim. The line did not stop at the edge. It ran on, out past the lath's tip, straight as a rule, and Ephram *knew* along it; whatever was going to cross that line, he had word of it a breath before it came. That was how a lath had been lying at the end of a lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:51:Ephram's heel went down for another cut out of the last, and it was the short one, with no hip in it. Cael had it a half beat early, as he had had it in the first exchange, and this time he went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:53:Eight. It was low, and inside, under the lath, along the line of Ephram's own arm, with the hip shrieking and the edge passing over his back. He came up off the landing beat with his right hand out, and laid two fingers on the inside of Ephram's wrist, on the tape, and gave it the spark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:55:It went out of him like a word, and the flint smell came up between them. Ephram yelped, and the lath jumped in his hand, and then he was laughing again, helplessly, shaking his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:67:Ephram stood at the far chalk, soaked and grinning, with a red mark on his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:71:So he would not chain. Cael read it in his weight before Rooke's hand had moved. Ephram was going to do the simplest thing a Blade could do: he would wait for a moment when there was only one place Cael could be, and then put everything he had into a single cut at that place, shoulders and all, the plain entry from the first year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:77:Cael went back before it. Nine. A burst along the wall, and the hip gave him nothing in return but pain. Ephram turned with him, unhurried, and the lines turned too, and he spent a tenth to stay ahead of them. He had never gone past nine on any floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:79:He put the push into the oak under the walking heel, and Ephram stepped over the place as a man steps over a puddle. It took the shoulder four to learn that he had seen it coming. He tried it a fifth time, at the load, at the edge of his reach, and the boards jumped a foot to the left of a boot that was no longer there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:81:Then the wall was at his back, with a flight of steps on his right hand, and a line lay on each side of him. Ephram stood ten feet off, and his heel was up. There was one place left, and both of them knew where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:He laid it out of the corner, down the only gap, fifteen feet along the foot of the wall. The dust woke in the joints and ran, and his ears closed and opened. It was a line a blind man could have read, and Ephram read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:93:He had a breath, perhaps, before the bill. He took the read back off the air and went the other way, a single stride, straight at the man, inside the arc of the cut, to the place where the lath's root would pass at the top of its swing, a foot from Ephram's own shoulder. He set his feet there. He put up his left forearm as a post, and he shut the give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:95:He sent the load to the floor. It was Brom's gift, the first thing he had ever taken from Brom, and he had watched it practised in a harness room for a year: nothing in the whole of him pushing back, only somewhere for the weight to go, down the forearm and the shoulder, down the back, through both heels into sprung oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:103:He heard it. He had heard the same sound once in a chalk cut, under the noise of Iron Skin holding: a click, and a give. Ephram's right arm stopped being a thing with an edge on it, and the lath fell out of his hand and rang on the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:Ephram went down on one knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:117:"Don't move it," said Brom. He was on his knees on the oak with one hand flat between Ephram's shoulder blades. "I've had one out. It goes back. It goes back worse if you help."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:119:"That's the door," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:123:"Every Blade's got a door in him. They tell you in the second year. They don't tell you where." Ephram opened his eyes, which were wet with pain, and quite clear. "I've wondered where mine was for three years. Nobody on this hill was ever early enough to stand in it." He breathed. "Did you know it would do that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:127:"Good," said Ephram. "I'd not have liked it if you'd known and done it anyway." He turned his head a little toward the stand. "Coach. Call it. I'm not having it set till it's called."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:133:Then the healer had Ephram's wrist in one hand and his elbow in the other, and Brom had him round the chest. She said something short, and there was a second sound like the first, and Ephram made a noise that he afterward denied. She bound his arm across his chest and said the shoulder would want a month.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:158:He knew this one's name, and where he slept, and what he whistled on a stair, and that he could not get the third line of a song right. Ephram was eighteen inches from him, having his arm bound, and he could have said it now. That was the difference, and it was the cost. He had written it down the night before at full weight, and it weighed exactly what he had written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:168:"Which name, Coach? It isn't a program bout, and it isn't a ladder bout. The house's permanent column only has a head for one fighter. Ephram, or the enrollee?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:170:Rooke did not turn round. He was watching Brom and the healer get Ephram to his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:188:The yard was a great deal of noise. He had expected that, and had not expected the kinds. The Current girl was standing on her bench with both fists in the air and tears running down her face, and seemed not to know about the tears. The assistant had her hands over her mouth. Half of Ephram's cohort were over the rail and round him, and the porter stood by his pail with his cap in his hand, blowing his nose and speaking severely to the ground. Brom walked beside the healer with the captain's good arm across his shoulders, and his eyes were wet, and he did not wipe them; he had said once that a man who looks at a hurt only teaches it to be looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:232:Ephram came back when the light had begun to go yellow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:236:It was the yard's custom, and he had watched it from a tier a hundred times and never stood in it: after a bout on the oak the clerk chalked the result, and the two who had fought stood at the board while he did. The first board was high. The clerk set the porter's ladder against it, and Cael steadied one side of it with his one good hand and Ephram the other with his, and neither of them said that it wanted steadying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:238:The clerk chalked the result under the notice in the same three-line grammar the yard used for every bout. Cael and Ephram stood together while he did it. Ephram looked at the board rather than at Cael, which was the register the ritual ran in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:246:Ephram would have listened. He would have gone still, and thought about it, and very probably said that he was glad of that as well. But he had just said, of his own accord, that he did not need to know; he had turned his back on a table of papers twice, for a friend's sake, on the same understanding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:252:"You've a look on you," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:256:"You always are." Ephram took his hand back and hitched the coat on his shoulder with it. "Don't make me sound clever in it. I walked into a post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:264:*Nine. Taken in earnest from a friend who asked me to show him straight — so I showed him straight, and the architecture did what it does with earnest, and the notice said "non-hostile," which it has never said, because there has never been a fight like it in the log. Ephram asked for the true measurement. He got it. He doesn't know he gave me a piece of himself for it; he consented to a fight, not to the thing the fight fed. I wrote the price down before the bout, the way the covenant says, so that when it cost — and it cost — I'd have written the cost before I collected it, which is the only line between me and what the office did at a road cut and in a records hall — spending people whose names it never bothered to write down, and asking nobody anything.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:276:It had lifted its head a dozen times in four exchanges. A heel going down on oak was the plainest thing to fix in all the world, and Ephram had put his heel down thirty times; one point under that boot, set and told to stay, would have ended the bout in the first exchange. *There?* it had asked, each time, in its courteous way. *His*, he had told it. *Leave it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:280:He shut the book and put it in the satchel, which Brom had left for him on the step with the strap done up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:282:He had gone out by the yard gate after Ephram left, to put his head under the pump in the covered walk. It was a plain stone gate with a dressed lintel, and he had walked beneath it a thousand times, and halfway through, something had raised its hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:5:He stood in the passage a moment with the satchel on his shoulder. It was past midnight, and the house was dark from the lodge to the library, and he had come down four flights and across the court to tell a woman a thing before a board could tell her. He had not thought about what he would say. He knocked on the frame, as he knocked on Brom's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:25:*Notice of date, to the Chancellor, by a district officer: upon the ruling, or the day after.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:53:"So I kept it," she said. "For four days, through a return and a ruling. I let you hear the hood say *revoked* without knowing how short the time was. I should like to say that it was easy and that I never wavered, and I can't; I sat in a carriage this afternoon and very nearly said it through the window." Her mouth moved a little. "I wanted it to be yours. If you went, I wanted you to go because you had priced it with your own people, at a table, with my need left out of the sum. And if you stayed, I meant to walk beside you into that frame-house and take what they wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:69:She laid one finger on the empty ruling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:71:"In the week after the close a clerk will come up our road with a stamp, and he will write here what the ruling says of the respondent institution. *Respondent institution compliant; no finding.* I shall give him tea. I told you a year ago that the best day this folder could have was a clerk writing *sustained*, and I was wrong, for this is better. It is the cleanest remedy column I have seen against a contested entry in twenty years at the registry and all my years in this chair." She took the finger away. "And in Bracken's enrollment book, under your name, there will be five words in his hand. Under three other names as well, I gather, though I have not asked how many and I shall not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:83:"There is no form for taking a leaf out of that folder," she said. "I told you so. There is also no rule against putting one in. I have a closing in the registry's presses under an enrollee's name, which I can cite, and a magister's note for the seventy-sixth day. I have thirty-four chancellors who wrote to ask me what I would do, and who now owe me the courtesy of reading what I did. And I have a right-hand column in a great many places besides this one." She came round the end of the desk. "I do not know what took you up that stair tonight with four others. I am not a fool, and I can see that it was more than a ruling, and I am not going to ask. Bracken asks when. I find I do not want even that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:113:*And this. He isn't to be told. Not tomorrow on the oak, nor in a letter from wherever we are by then. He's the one person on this hill who wanted the true measurement and nothing else, and he's waited three years for it, and I'm going to take something from him in the middle of it and never say. Lira's known since Ardenmere. Brom, Karis. The one I took the quiet thing from knows now. He can't. It would put him on a file beside mine, and he's staying.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:119:Brom's lamp was lit. He was sitting on the edge of his bed in his shirt with his forearms on his knees, and on the table under the window lay two papers. On the left was the district's finding, folded along its folds, with the printed slip tucked into it; on the right, a hand's breadth off, lay the envelope with the red seal, as he had squared it four weeks ago, and its seal had not so much as a crack across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:121:"I said I'd know what I was answering," said Brom, when he saw where Cael was looking. "I do. I find I needn't read the question." He took the book and read the leaf through at his own pace, and set his mark at the foot, the B with the bar. "They can lie there, the two of them. I've signed nothing and I've refused nothing. Somebody will find them when the room's cleared and wonder." He gave the book back. "He'll hit you hard. He hits harder than he looks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:123:Karis was still in the reading room, on the floor, with a lantern. She read the leaf twice, and at *I'll price it as if it didn't* she made a small sound through her nose and wrote *K.* under Brom's mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:127:At the sill again he turned back a leaf and wrote the day's line under the ruling's three words, because it would not wait for morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:149:She was going to walk out of this room standing up, and nobody was putting her out. The door was open, and there was a sitting with her name fourth on a board, and a coach who had ruled a line under it so that it could not be wiped off. She could stay. She had turned that over on a stair in the records hall, honestly, watching Brom weigh paper: she could let four people go east, and be at a blue door in a fortnight in clean wraps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:175:She took the pen from where it had rolled against the clock. Under Brom's barred letter and Karis's neat one she wrote *L.* Then she turned the pen in her fingers, and wrote one line more, across the foot, in the capitals she kept for such leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:207:Cael watched it from the north wall, because there was nothing else he could usefully do. The ferry came across low in the water and went back empty and came again. The bluff road had people on every switchback: wool-factors in their good coats, and carters, and a file of district clerks who had found that they had business on the hill. The man who had bought a second press was there, and so was the woman who kept the guesting-house, with a basket. The lake house's blue-grey wagon had not gone home after the ruling, and it came up at a walk with a lath in a canvas sleeve sticking out of the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:209:The lodge-man had his book out on a trestle under the arch and was making every one of them sign it. *Callers, sign and wait*, said his board, and he had kept that gate since before Bracken, and saw no reason why a ruling should alter it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:215:*Sixth-day. The Crown yard. Ephram. All of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:229:He found his own people without looking for them. They were on the east tier, low down: Brom, with his arms folded, and Karis beside him with her hands under her, and Lira between the Current girl and the Stone. The assistant sat behind them, and behind her were two benches of Ephram's cohort, who had been told nothing about cheering today. At the end of the stand, by the porter's boards, where the year's names stood in their columns, a man in a dark coat stood with his hands in his pockets, and nobody would have looked at him twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:235:Ephram was on the oak already. He stood in the middle of it in his greys with the lath in his right hand, point down, and when Cael came off the steps he turned and walked to a chalk. He did not go to the south one. It was the fourth bell, with the sun come round over the lecture range, and from the south chalk a man had it at his back. Ephram walked the whole length of the floor to the north mark and turned there, and stood with the light full in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:239:He was leaving himself no excuse. Cael understood it as he walked to the south chalk with the sun warm between his shoulders: whatever Ephram learned in the next four exchanges, he meant to have learned it against every disadvantage he could arrange, so that there would be nothing afterward to set against it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:249:Ephram came off the north mark at a walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:251:Cael knew the walk. He had stood in front of it for a season on this floor, calling the weight aloud: every angle, every pace, the lath low and never thrown. The old read went out through his boots into sprung oak and came back with Ephram in it, early, as it always did. The weight was forward and a little left, the shoulders had not dropped, and the back heel was down. It was the front foot only, then, and the thing to do was wait.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:255:It did not stop, and that was new. The first cut had not finished before Ephram's front heel was down again, a pace on, and a second cut was already beginning out of the end of the first, a different way in, flat, at the ribs. Heel, hip, edge. He could not take the first one back and had not tried to; he had only built the second on top of it, as a mason lays a course.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:267:Heel, and then the edge. Ephram had cut the middle out of it, and the lath was simply there, a beat sooner than three years of watching had taught Cael's eyes to expect, and it lay across his left forearm from the wrist to the elbow like a bar of cold iron. Then it was hot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:273:Ephram did not lift the lath in salute. He looked at Cael along it for a moment, with the sun in his eyes and no pleasure at all in his plain face, and then he turned and walked back to the north chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:281:Ephram did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:285:"That's ninety-nine," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:305:The Wind first, because it was the oldest: low and flat off the left foot, straight down the middle of the floor. The oak took the landing and threw him on, as it was built to, and he was in the air again before the beat had finished. Three. Ephram's hands had come off the grip, and he had the lath up, and his heel was going down, and the read had all of it, deep, to the hips, a full beat before it happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:309:It was not a flat hand, which was all he had ever shown on the bluff. This was the push at fighting load, the whole shove of it, through his palm and down, and the air over a square yard of floor went solid and then gave. Ephram's front heel came down on a board that had jumped, and the first beat of his cut landed crooked. A Blade could not take it back. The hip followed the crooked heel, and the edge followed the hip, wide, a yard from anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:313:Four. He went past the wide cut on the outside, and Ephram turned on the miss faster than a man of his size ought to turn. A line was already there. He had not cut it; he had turned his wrist, and the lath lay level across the place where Cael's next landing would come down, because he read lines, and knew what would cross one a breath before it crossed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:317:Ephram was grinning. Cael saw it, across the floor, with the sun full on it, and he had never seen that face grin on the oak in three years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:321:He had done it on a stone floor at Norhold with eight thousand people watching, and on a chalk road, and in a painted hall at night, and he had never done it here. He set his feet and asked, and a lane went down across the Crown yard from his chalk to the space behind Ephram's left shoulder, fifteen feet of it, shoulder-wide. His ears closed and opened. The dust in the joints of the oak woke and ran, pale, along the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:329:He saw it with a beat to spare, and understood it in the same instant. A lane was a line. It was the longest, plainest line anybody had ever laid on that oak, with dust running down it to show the way, and Ephram had not needed to know what it was. He had read where it went, and had put his edge at the end of it and stood there, waiting, with his heel down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:337:Ephram did not know about the ten counts; nobody on the bluff did but three people on a tier. But he was the best Iron on the hill and he could see a man who had gone a beat late, and he came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:347:Ephram turned and came on, breathing through his mouth. He had the sun behind him now and Cael had it in his eyes; they had changed ends without either of them choosing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:355:He set his feet, and Ephram set his. The lath came up, and the heel began to go down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:363:Ephram lowered the lath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:7:"Junior," said the senior chair. He had the minute open in front of him and was running his thumb down the fresh page. "This wants to be in the porch before the ruling. That's the code, for a return. I've a tooth that won't let me climb, and the clerk's gone to his dinner. Take it up to the loft and stand over them while they copy it. Fair, one sheet, and you compare it yourself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:63:There was nobody on the steps. A boy came across from the trough, one of the lads the correspondents paid to watch the board, and read the heading with his lips moving: *Return of the evaluation seat.* He looked for a ruling under it and did not find one, and went back to the trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:105:*Relief sought upon.* He considered that for some time, and wrote *The conclusion of the proceedings by ruling.* It was tomorrow, and a man might fairly ask to be relieved of a duty on the day the duty ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:121:The bridge-ward had lit his lantern. Havel sat at the window with his aching hand open on his knee and watched it, and thought, a little, about the young man at the far table, who had sat up straight for a year. He did not know what a ruling would do to him. He had never known what any of it would do; that had not been his to know. He found, sitting there, that he hoped the registers would be kind to him. He knew of no register that entered kindness, and he hoped it all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:129:They were at the head of the market street, the two of them, at the second bell on the last Fifth-day, where the cobbles began to fall toward the river. Below on the left were the four steps of the wool exchange, with people gathering on them already for a ruling that was hours off. Along a side street to the right, three corners down, a blue door stood in a plain stone front with a board beside it, and Brom and Lira were walking toward it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:131:Cael watched them go, because he could not go with them. He was a respondent on a call day, and though the district hall was well within the bell, a respondent did not walk into another proceeding's room on the morning of his own ruling; the house's counsel had said so, and then had said that she was sorry. So he stood with the registrar and saw a broad back in a good coat and a narrow one beside it go down a street. Brom walked as he walked to a frame, and Lira had her hands in her pockets and was, for once, not talking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:141:Then Lira came round the corner at something that was nearly a run, and stopped herself, and walked, and Brom came after her at his own pace. He looked just as he had looked going in, with his coat buttoned, except that there were two red bars across his forearms where the cuffs had ridden up, darker than the wool press had left them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:147:"I held it." He thought about how to say the rest. "It was more than Ephram ever put on, a good deal over. They let it down on springs, and then they let it down again while I had it, which he never did, and I heard the middle one say *forty* and go on counting." He looked at his forearms. "The plate lit. I felt it under my boots. It's warm. I didn't know they were warm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:151:"They don't tell you. They write. It goes to the registrar by the bag." Brom glanced at Bracken, and Bracken inclined his head. "Three of them at a table, and a clerk, and a frame the size of a hay wagon. Nobody said a word to me but *stand there* and *thank you*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:155:Brom did not say anything to that. He stood in the market street with his forearms marked and his face quite calm, and Cael saw that it had been enough. Whatever a clerk wrote on whatever paper, a plate had come up warm under his boots and said what was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:163:They were standing along the walls, and the doors had been left open so that the steps could hear, and the steps were full, and past them the street. Everybody who had come down for the closing had come again, and Ephram's cohort had the ninth and tenth benches, and this time nobody had needed to threaten them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:173:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. For ruling. The fourteenth sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:223:"I want one thing said before the hill gets hold of it," she said. "I read losing filings for twenty years at the registry. It was my work to say why they had lost. They lost because a date was wrong, or a section was misread, or somebody had hoped a seat would not notice a thing it was paid to notice." She looked at Karis, and then, longer, at Cael. "You did not lose badly. I should like that to be the first thing either of you remembers about today, and I shall say it to anybody who asks me. There is not a line in that ruling that answers a line of yours. It could not answer them. It went round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:235:He rubbed out the three lines with the heel of his hand, the two closings and the ruling, and left the fourth, because that was Brom's and not the house's. He wrote in their place, square and plain:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:241:"Copy-sets," he said, to Cael, who had not asked. "The Chancellor's order, from the carriage. Thirty-four houses wrote to ask what this one would do if the seat went against it. They are to have the ruling in the house's own fair copy by tomorrow's first bag, with the standing entries, and no gloss. Three leaves to a set, and five sets more for the faculties." He pulled down his second glasses. "Thirty-nine. I have told the kitchen to send bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:243:Seln was at the counter's far end. He had come down in the coach's corner without a word, and he stood now under the counter lamp with the enrollee's copy of the ruling laid in front of him on the wood. It was the seat's own engrossment, on registry stock, with the wax of the river and the scale at its foot; Cael had carried it up the hill in the satchel and had not been able to think what to do with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:265:At the middle table the copy-sets were coming off the clerks' pens, and Brom was squaring them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:271:"This one's light," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:277:The clerk took it, and counted, and counted again more slowly with a wet thumb. There were two; the standing entries had been left out of it. He went red and reached for a clean sheet, and Brom took up the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:281:Lira was on the stair. It was the narrow stair behind the counter, the one that went up to Bracken's rooms, and she sat on its fourth step with her knees drawn up and her chin on them, in the shadow above the lamps. She had not said a word since the coach, and she was watching Brom's hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:9:Karis had the table's end. Lira sat on the bench with her back to the wall and one boot up, and Brom was in the good chair from the dairy, which had somehow followed them up four flights months ago and never gone down. Seln came last, on the tenth bell, and shut the door behind him without a sound. He had his post satchel over his shoulder, and when he sat he did not hang it on the chair but set it on the floor between his feet, upright, and Cael heard the weight of what was in it meet the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:"The ruling," said Karis. "Everybody heard it, and I've the engrossment by heart, so I'll give it once and short. Lawful. Compliant. Revoked from the year's close. A frame within five weeks, on a date they'll send, and the fourteenth chapter when the frame returns nothing." She laid her pale fingers on the three slips. "That leaves three roads, and I've written one on each. I haven't put them in order of anything, because I didn't want to cheat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:25:Brom leaned forward in the good chair with his forearms on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:39:"With no date. Withrow underlined that twice in her own code, nine years apart. And three is part nine." He had it by heart, having heard Bracken read it, flat, on the night of the procedure. "They may restrict, freeze or hold anything that attaches to me: a record, a sponsorship, a travel paper, *any institutional relationship.* They've walked those fences already. They read Lira's file for completeness, and they wrote to Brom's family's estate, and they queried Karis's standing twice, and they sent two polite men to Denvash to ask about a shop's title."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:51:"That's not a price," said Brom slowly. "That's a no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:63:"We have the thing that ends it," said Karis. "It's between Seln's feet. I spent fifty-one minutes last week telling a seat that its instrument had failed to read him, and I couldn't say why. I know why. I can date it to within a renewal of a charter. That isn't an argument from discretion; it's the newest matter anybody has put before the registry in two hundred years, and the code gives an appeal from a seat's ruling upon new matter. I looked it up on the night we came home from the close, because I couldn't sleep. It goes to the convening, within thirty days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:89:Brom had been listening with his head down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:113:"Brom first," said Lira. "He's got the most paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:115:"Mine's on my table," said Brom. "Under a whetstone, and beside it. I can tell you what it is without fetching it." He rubbed his forearms, where the red bars were. "There's a finding coming by the bag. I stood for it this morning and I'm not sorry. And there's an envelope that I said I'd know how to answer once the ruling was in. Well, the ruling's in. So—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:129:Brom had got to his feet. He took the packet, and looked at Bracken, and Bracken nodded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:131:There were two papers in it. The first was a full sheet under the district's seal, and Brom read it standing, without moving his lips, and then aloud, slowly, testing each plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:133:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. The panel, having read the record and the house's sheets, and having taken a reading in the sigil frame under load, finds for the tier. Iron. Rank to be entered upon the practitioner's signature before the registrar of his house, within fourteen days.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:139:Brom did not answer, for he was looking at the second paper. It was a printed slip, a third the size of the sheet, with a narrow margin and a rule at its head, and Cael, from the bench, could read its heading upside down: *Guidance to the practitioner upon advancement.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:143:Brom read it, and this time he did not read it aloud. He read it twice, by the movement of his eyes, and then he held it out across the table, not to Cael but to Karis, because she was the one who read such things, and she read it to the room in her small dry voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:153:Brom stood with the finding in one hand. He was a large man, and he looked for a moment as he had looked under a wool press, with a load come down on him all at once that was a little over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:171:Nobody said anything. Lira had both hands over her mouth. Cael found that he could not see the ruled column very well, and bent his head over it, and wrote under Brom's name what Brom had said, word for word, because he had promised to write what they told him and not what he guessed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:221:"*Assistant, the: an enrollee of the second year. Not mine to forfeit, and so a letter, which I have written, to the research council. It says that the line they voted is for research, enrolled, standing, and that she is all three, and can find a section from a struck heading; and it asks that the room and the line pass to her.*" She paused. "*A second letter, to the assistant herself: not written. I don't know how to mark it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:231:She laid the sheet on the bare cloth, beside Brom's finding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:247:"And you," said Brom. "You've a line with three words on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:255:"Ephram," said Brom, and got up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:259:Brom was gone a quarter of an hour, and the bell for midnight went over the court while he was on the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:Ephram came in as he had come in on a birthday, a little out of breath, with the captain's file under his arm in its green tape, and he had plainly not been asleep. He stopped inside the door, and his eyes went along the table from end to end as they went along a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:279:"Say it anyway," said Ephram. "I'd rather have it said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:287:"Past the Line, then." Ephram stood with his hand on the file. "Then nobody will ever write a figure for you again. Not Gault, nor a panel, nor me." He was quiet for the space of two breaths. "I thought it might be that. I've thought so since the boxes went onto Bracken's shelf; I've eyes, and I walk the records hall on Third-days. I didn't know it was all of you." He looked at Brom, who had come in behind him, and Brom looked back, and whatever passed between the two of them had been settled in a harness room over a rack of iron plates and did not need a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:289:"I'm not coming," said Ephram. "You haven't asked, and I'd like it said before you feel you ought to. This is my hill. I've a cohort, and a column, and next year's first-years who'll want somebody to tell them the rota's a joke. I'm made for a place with a board in it." He said it without any apology. "I think you aren't. I think I've known that for longer than you have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:303:"I told you on that oak, at the end of your first year, that I'd learn what you were the only honest way there is," said Ephram. "With a floor, and somebody at the rail. I've had ninety-eight lessons since in what you'll let a table see. I've a chart of them, and it's the best-kept record of a thing not happening that anybody ever made." His voice was quite level. "So here's what I've been keeping. I want one bout. The Crown yard, tomorrow, under Rooke's rule, in front of whoever comes. And I want all of it. Not the ceiling. I ruled that ceiling, and I know to the half point what's under it. I want whatever's over it." He put two fingers on the red line. "If you give me the band I shall put my lath down and stand there till the glass runs out, in front of the whole house, and you can explain it to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:307:"I told you at the trial that whatever you are, I was glad it was on my side of the floor," said Ephram. "I said some hard things on a tier in your first months here, and I've been glad every day since that I was wrong. I'd like to find out whether I'm still glad when you stop being careful with me. I think I will be. I'd like to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:311:Two years ago this was the worst thing that could have happened to him on the bluff. He had built a band and a rotation and a variance so that nobody would ever stand where Ephram was standing and say what Ephram had just said. He waited for the old cold to come up through his stomach, and it did not come. There was a ruling in his satchel, and a slip on the table with one word on it. The band had been rent paid on a room, and he was leaving the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:313:And Ephram had asked. He had asked of his own accord, in earnest, for a fight. Lira's head had come up at that, for she knew what such an asking meant under a rule with four sets of initials, and so did Brom, and Karis had gone very still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:317:"I thought it mightn't be." Ephram gathered the three sheets, and folded them, and did not tie the tape. "I'll be on the landing. I'll have my back to the door." He went out, and they heard him cross the boards and stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:333:Brom's hand went up first, as it had in this room once before, the right, flat, like a man taking an oath. Lira's followed, and then Karis's, pale to the second knuckle, and Cael put up his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:339:"Five," said Brom. He counted them again, though the first count had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:341:Lira went to the door and opened it. Ephram was standing at the head of the stair with his back to her, looking down four flights into the dark, with the green file under his arm.

