+- Fragment count is correct at this stage: Anchor is the eighth confirmed (ch32:25–40; ch34:223–235), and the still place remains explicitly uncounted. The anomaly has not been promoted.
+- Knowledge boundary still holds through M5. Seln decides to open his case after recognizing the fourth grade / hand (ch29:43–103), but ch34 ends before the annex disclosure. He knows the service system and that Cael has concealed something from his returns; he has not yet learned Shadow’s mechanism or why it is sealed.
+- Velmere day-45 envelope remains sealed and physically intact after the road; Brom checks the left inside pocket (ch29:217–249; ch32:50–65).
+- The ch33 doorway event is Anchor’s one uncommanded fixing, not the legal “clean door.” The latter is the fourth-sitting departure window, later fixed explicitly at day 75→115 (ch38:165–203).
+- Reader Standard holds through the complex Anchor fight because each abstraction is demonstrated physically before Cael names it. The one dense passage is ch31:191–227 (cage, moving stitch, lines through the fourteen), but the unthreading action immediately verifies the model.
+- Pacing: ch28–29 spend a long runway on paper provenance and Seln’s catechism, but both are required to make his case-opening choice credible. Ch33–34 give a deliberately full two-chapter recovery; the doorway accident, naming solution, cost curve, birthday room, three lost bouts and Hesk’s satchel all earn space individually, though the birthday chapter repeats the “good room / turned paper / nobody asks” emotional grammar at length.
+- Calendar control holds: ch34:21’s “the eleventh of Sowing” is the one fixed-date exception expressly preserved by OWNER #42. It is not paired with a season word or a month-of-year ordinal.
+- Texture watch: the book now has many scenes where a meaningful object is placed/squared, everyone is silent, and a final plain sentence interprets the silence. Also count “quietly,” “for a long time/moment,” “the way a man/person…,” quarter-turns, and “nobody said/asked.”
+
+### Movement 6 — chapters 35–40
+
+- The knowledge boundary lands exactly where required. Seln opens the case in M6 (ch35:153–191), the group reads station readings/orders, reports, memoranda and the advisory leaf before drawing conclusions (ch35:195–293; ch36:3–123), and Seln states the downward corrective practice once the paper supports it (ch36:127–173). Only after that does Cael disclose the fragments and Shadow’s origin/reason for sealing (ch37:1–208).
+- Shadow terms are exact and earned: Seln receives the whole story, including why his trade made public use traceable (ch37:119–178); the debt closes but Seln neither releases nor reclaims the fragment (ch37:188–202); his seal is “while its use can be seen by anyone who could carry the seeing west” and otherwise Cael’s without asking permission (ch37:198–208). The final inventory revision says “Sealed, on his terms” (ch37:250–268).
+- “Twice more” remains exactly two at ch37:97–107 and is repeated as two at ch37:258–262. No drift.
+- Kindling mechanics are finally and clearly paid out in the correct movement: the person’s Arbiter wakes and the station’s plate/reading apparatus records (Cael’s remembered Denvash at ch36:113–123); the advisory corrections function is appended to, not identical with, the reading (ch36:83–123); station keeper margins preserve reach before the entry order removes them (ch35:195–217; ch39:29–47); Cael’s eleven seconds are the function failing safe because there is no column / all margin (ch39:51–67). The distinction is followable on one read because the paper examples precede the theory.
+- The case respects reserved truth limits. Karis dates the policy to about two hundred years and establishes an older reading plus the former “Unbound” door (ch39:91–143), then explicitly enters as a limit everything below the case: what the reading is, who made it, how old it is. Session nine/anomaly remains untouched and uncounted (ch39:159–169).
+- Anchor perception locks are exact: sure/about ten feet, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen (ch38:317–349). The trial distinguishes perception from reach and stops at the first near-fix, with Brom’s count remaining one (ch38:351–379). No fixing is normalized.
+- Clean-door calendar is explicit: fourth sitting closed day 75; absent becomes fled day 115 (ch38:165–203). This confirms the 75→115 lock. The annex disclosure is day 88 (ch37:250–262), and Greyvane leaves arrive day 91 (ch38:129–145).
+- Hesk’s clock still loses time (ch35:99–103). Gault remains evaluator of record in the earlier gate record and no later office drift appears. Velmere envelope remains sealed in Brom’s left pocket at ch36:127–139.
+- Arc turn fully pays: “the room was never the system” becomes “the honest people were in the drawer too” (ch35:271–291), then “it isn’t broken; it’s maintained” (ch39:191–219). Lira’s reframing of the cache as a map rather than evidence (ch39:229–253) converts revelation into the next movement’s action logic.
+- Vastin’s borrowed-room read earns its substantial length. Each of the four proposed risks is honored; he independently refuses verification, report and certification (ch40:201–219), refuses false alliance (ch40:221–247), and later lives the cost by leaving the northern girl’s file in observation rather than exposing her (ch40:251–329). His refusal to certify becomes a future causal lever, not consolation.
+- Reader Standard holds despite density because the narrative repeatedly distinguishes reading / function / entry with concrete paired sheets and explains why every available verification instrument is compromised. Nothing is unsuitable for thirteen.
+- Texture/padding watch: ch35–40 contain many variations of a bare/borrowed/good room, paper laid in rows, prolonged collective silence, hands flat on tables, and readers taking documents in order. Most carry character/epistemic meaning, but the cumulative ritual is audible; trim only non-protected connective repetitions, never the disclosure units.
+- No definite continuity failure found through M6.
+
+### Movement 7 — chapters 41–47
+
+- The recess does narrative work instead of suspending the argument. Cael chooses to keep making the Compact rule in its own hand without pretending the room is just (ch41:145–191); Lira restarts the contested ladder on her own terms (ch42:223–277; ch43:237–277); Seln turns road knowledge into practical instruction for all five (ch42:83–191); Karis and Brom separately begin making their work portable (ch46:81–119; ch47:117–159, 185–219).
+- Calendar locks remain internally exact. The sixth sitting’s re-tendered schedule is day 117 (ch44:141–203); the court sets two closings and the ruling five weeks out (ch47:91–101); Brom’s panel and the ruling share the last Fifth-day (ch47:103–113, 163–179). The phrases “the year’s last month” and “last Fifth-day” are approved #42 landmarks, not forbidden month order.
+- Office and address locks hold. The side-door log remains *Caelen Hesk-ward* (ch44:19–21), Gault is repeatedly *evaluator of record* (ch44:67–95; ch46:3–35), and the requested certificate is explicitly from the Archmarshal’s office (ch46:253–285; ch47:13).
+- The merits sequence sharpens the book’s thesis. Jent concedes renewals, the nine-day point and Gault’s honest figures because none reaches the discretionary frame (ch44:53–81). Gault then gets the day-76 riders into the permanent minute under the seat’s own order, and it changes nothing (ch46:35–65). Honest paper can survive while institutional consequence is designed away.
+- Havel’s private six-sheet boundary holds: he recognizes the unexplained designation but does not invent a seventh sheet because the two filed copies answer the narrow records question (ch44:141–207). This preserves his established ethic and does not spend the designation’s reserved origin.
+- The day-45 Velmere envelope remains unbroken after eighteen weeks and is moved, still sealed, from Brom’s coat to his table (ch47:131–159). He explicitly will not open it before panel or ruling. No identity drift.
+- Capability/count continuity holds: honest-floor perception remains ten feet / faint twelve (ch47:19); Shadow stays sealed during Seln’s night-road lesson (ch42:143–179); Lira knowingly exploits the ten-count lane bill (ch45:253–281); Anchor’s new fixing is refused by naming Lira’s feet (ch45:285–297). Hesk’s clock loses its usual minutes (ch41:57–61; ch46:117–119; ch47:263).
+- Lira’s sequence earns its room. Her Current loss identifies the right-foot dependency and Cael’s failure to tell her (ch43:75–231); the Blade rematch turns touch-and-lift into choice (ch45:83–171); the private unbanded fight lets her see Cael’s true timing and take one touch by spending his known bill (ch45:203–325). The three scenes change ownership of knowledge each time rather than replaying a training beat.
+- Brom’s frame practice likewise earns length: the left shoulder’s deficit is identified, loaded and restored through eleven trials (ch45:33–79), while his reason for submitting to the panel—wanting to be right about himself before an honest checker—makes the later refusal consequential rather than automatic.
+- Reader Standard holds. Even the legal concession chapter translates its effect into the shrinking corridor / cleared table image (ch44:71–83). No material unsuitable for thirteen.
+- Padding/texture watch: the closing half of M7 repeatedly stages documents or boxes squared to table/shelf edges, hands on pockets, meaningful silence, and a final understated sentence explaining mutual knowledge (ch44:101–137; ch46:81–169; ch47:117–225). Character motifs justify several instances, but the sheer cross-chapter concentration should be a texture-pass target.
+- No cross-movement continuity or knowledge-boundary defect found in M7.
